The large language model (LLM) sphere is abuzz with the November 2022 announcement of the release of LLaMA, an 8B and 65B parameter pre-trained language model developed by the researchers at the Meta AI team. Developed as an advancement of their efforts with OPT and aims to contribute to the flourishing open research community of LLM development that until now, has been mostly fueled by large enterprises. Fully trained task and Causal Language Model (CLM) fine-tuned models are now available so researchers can experiment with it according to their needs.

What is LLaMA and why was it developed?

Large Language Models (LLM) aren’t a new invention, but they’re increasingly popular in the AI community, especially after the announcement of ChatGPT, GPT-3.5 and BERT languages have been trained using billions of words with parameters ranging anywhere between 300M to 530B. Using these huge amounts of data that is fed to LMs needs optimized and scalable computing. And, to build such a complex model, companies have to spare vast amounts of computing resources and dedicate a lot of expense to the engineering and development needed.

Another major setback for experimentation by the scientific community in LLMs is licensing of models. Companies tend to prohibit open usage, hence it restricts research and growth in this direction, as the cost for access becomes too expensive for most researchers. Data sharing is also a problem where machine translation doesn’t get the needed exposure to make them evolve.

There was a dire need to develop a model that not only trained at a large scale and was cheap to train by breaking this barrier and could be a candidate for scaling up to hundreds of billions of parameters and be available freely for research. LLaMA was the answer to this need. It is not only open source and proved to be impressively powerful, but also supports 20 programming languages, can be efficient on smaller GPU machines and has achieved SOTA (state-of-the-art) performance on zero-shot and few-shot reasoning benchmarks, including:

- XSum (test set, AMR metric) with 17.02% rouge-1 score
- Circa (dev set) with 74.02% exact match score
- MMLU (test set) with 61.2% score
- BIG-Bench-HARD (BBH) with 68.2% score

Applying multilingual frozen pre-trained LMs to machine translation

LLaMa is released as a frozen LM, which means it can be applied directly to new tasks without additional fine-tuning. This can result in two stages of training – in the first phase, you train an adaptor (or adapter) which is a lightweight language translation layer that maps representations without fine-tuning but trained from different LMs without zero-shot resorting to translate to a target language, and in the second phase, you perform the final inferences.

Image Source: https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.09219

For its simplicity, flexibility, and ability to use frozen LMs, this can be extended to machine translations. This opens up a world of possibilities, allowing LMs to be used for tasks such as cross-lingual transfer and multilingual translation, without the need to train models from scratch.

The future of LLaMA in the AI and ML research communities

LLaMa is a major breakthrough in the field of NLP and large scale pre-trained models. The open sourcing of the model and its datasets will make this resource available for research to greater numbers of people, and likely lead to even more innovations in the field. Its impact will be felt in many different areas, but particularly in the application of language models to areas such as machine translation and cross-lingual transfer learning.

Not only does LLaMA enable research at a large scale, but it is also easily extensible for various tasks and comes unfrozen so it’s easier to use and experiment. Meta AI is also offering deep learning infrastructure that allows others to replicate the work and understand the model so it’s easily duplicated. LLaMA is open to all domains and tasks, not just language-based ones. Being so customizable and accessible, it is believed that this will also spur a race to build related infrastructure and keep optimizing LLaMa. Right now, Meta AI feels they need more resources and research to actually make if production ready but once that’s done, it
