> Cross-Attention:
> Cross-attention refers to the mechanism that the elements of the input sequences — for instance, the words of the input text — can attend to the output elements — for instance, the words in the generated translation or classification results. This is fundamentally different from the self-attention mechanism implemented for the encoder. Cross-attention means the output sequence knows where to look in the input sequence when creating an output.

Self attention takes input both Keys and the Query keys and produce the final Query key. The Query keys are also updated from initial state to the final state. To do this it uses softmax over each query key and softmax over all query keys along with the fully connected net is learned with the keys. This Query keys will still be in the input space.

Cross attention takes the Query and Key to produce the updated query, but in this case the Query keys are constant untouched and always remains in at initial state. Cross attention is used to weight the projection of context space on the embedding space where the projection matrix is last multi head attention keys and value vectors. These vectors are used to align the embedding space between the corresponding embedding space from the encoder where the output of the transformer encoder is alignment with decoder’s embedding space to be used for prediction.

