🧠 LLM Response:
 How does a tokenizer work? Can you write a function to tokenize a list of words? Briefly explain the three methods used for creating word vectors. Write a program to represent the words "machine", "machinery", "mechanical", and "language" using representatives such as vectors, matrices, and tensors.

In natural language processing (NLP), tokenization is a process to divide a given text(document) into smaller chunks called tokens. These tokens are used to represent the text in NLP models. A token could be a word, character, or syllable. Depending upon specific tasks, a tokenizer can break the text into any of these forms. There are several ways by which we can tokenize the words:

- White space Tokenization: This is the most basic and popular technique used for tokenizing the given text base on white space present. This technique is followed by breaking the formed words and removing the white spaces among them.
- Subword Tokenization: In Natural language, same word can act differently in different context, Suppose in a sentence like "I order to eat." here the word 'order' will play the role of verb, while in a sentence like "In order to eat." the same word will be used as preposition. But the subword tokenizer uses to break into smaller and common units used across different contexts.
- Character Tokenization: This is the most basic method of tokenizing a text by representing every character of the text as a separate token. The downside of this method is that words with different lengths will have different sizes of vectors.

We can use the package called "nltk" to perform tokenization in python and here is a simple snippet of the code is given below:

> import nltk
> from nltk.tokenize import word_tokenize
> word_tokens = word_tokenize(“I love NLP using Python”)
> print(word_tokens)
> output: [‘I’, ‘love’, ‘NLP’, ‘using’, ‘Python’]

There are several methods that, each expecting input as a text document and returns its vector form, can be used to represent words as a vector:

1. Count Vector: This method is based on Bags of Words(BoW), the simplest method to transform text into vector form. This can be achieved by counting the occurrences of each word in texts and giving the words higher weightage if that word occurs in a document significantly.
2. TF-IDF: The working principle of TF-IDF is more or less similar to the above CountVector, the difference being the effect of the rare and common words. It discards the common than rare words in the corpus. The words that appear in rare corpuses get a higher weightage than the words which are frequency and common, thus providing better document distinction. The TF-IDF measures how relevant is the word to a document in the corpus, thereby considering the words in the corpus.
3. One-Hot Vectors: One-hot encoded vectors are preferred when the text vector can fit into memory as this method creates a vector of very big size in memory. This method gives a vector whose size is same in length of the vocabulary. Then the value of 1 is assigned at the index of the token and rest indexes will be zero.

Bag Of Words(BOW) is same as vectors, where each word is represented as word vector, matrix is a collection of several word vectors and tensors are used with neural networks such as CNN or LSTM in documentation for a better result.

