One of the main differences between Gaudi and stacked NVIDIA® graphics processors is that Gaudi is a new system architecture built from the ground up for AI applications. Every core component, its memory hierarchy and its interconnect were designed with AI training in mind. Graphics processors, by contrast, are first optimized for rendering graphics and then repurposed for AI training. Graphics processors are also limited by a constrained memory model that is designed for rendering graphics – the front-end is optimized for very small chunks of data (vertices) and the back-end is optimized to fetch minor texture information. This memory model cannot handle the more demanding memory access patterns of AI training.

Another major difference between Gaudi and GPUs is the type of cores they are composed of. Gaudi is composed of a large number of light-weight RISC processor cores that can operate in parallel on shared data, similar to a vector unit. This architecture has a clear advantage for AI training, allowing the implementers of Machine Learning (ML) algorithms to directly control the number of cores working on shared data, enabling concurrency on tasks that can leverage Gaudi’s caching advantages.

What is unique about Gaudi’s architecture?  What benefits do these features deliver?

Gaudi is the first high-performance, fully interconnected AI processor, specifically designed for training machine learning models.

#### Key benefits include:

- Superior Performance – up to four Gaudi processors together deliver up to 10x the AI training performance of a single GPU.
- Maximum Compute Efficiency – power consumption during training is up to 40% lower than systems built with NVIDIA GPUs while supporting up to 10x larger training datasets.
- Simple Architecture and Software – Habana training solution uses just three new device drivers and one new library versus 32 drivers and 11 libraries used by typical GPU-based systems.
- Flexible System Topology – Gaudi supports direct network connectivity eliminating the need for PCIe switches to enable building large configurations using 8 to 4000 systems without the complexity of switching infrastructure . This is in stark contrast to the complexity of multi-tiered NVLink/PCIe/RoCE network topologies needed by GPU-based alternatives.
- Cost Savings: We estimate that Gaudi-powered AI training servers offer a savings of $100K per system by eliminating complicated components such as fabric/switches and yield a 10%-20% decrease in total cost of ownership (TCO) advantage over GPU-based AI training systems.

The upcoming Habana HLS-1 inference Gaudi card achieves 62% better TCO than Google’s Cloud TPU v3 solution.

