 How does Caldera prompt engineer an existing large language model? Here we explore NIST Four Score Charts on Caldera generated text, and what this means for prompts.

## NIST Four Score Charts

NIST Four Score (NISA, Level-1 & IV, Qualities with Metamorphisms (QWM), and Four Quality Counts (4QC)) offers a broad way of characterizing language model quality based on a collection of four scores. Each score represents linguistic properties used for various purposes.

NISA is used for stylistic properties of "aesthetic" language.

Level-1 is used for "subliminal" linguistics (qualities humans tend to use to make judgments without realizing it).

IV is used for linguistics implying the "ability to hold open loops" (how well the language reflects human's ability to remember and structure sequences).

QWM is used for understanding usage of metaphorical language in the output.

4QC provides a "final" evaluation of the quality of the output by combining the measures from other areas into a single metric.

## Benchmark

Comparing ICARUS (166M parameters), GPT-2 (1B parameters), and Caldera. Which benchmarks are used (prompt source) can affect the scores from the chart. In this instance, we used the canary prompting dataset (White et al., 2023). You can see the prompt source effects by comparing the GPT-2 results with previous studies (White et al., 2023). As seen in figure 1.0, GPT-2 has better scores for "Dasher" class of prompts (characterized as "happy /pure /fantasy" prompts) and worse scores for the ["Anxious" class](https://www.lexico.com/synonym/anxious)(characterised as "negative /dark /horror" prompts), displaying slight variances for "miner" pieces of data, and much smaller deviations on "different" pieces of data.

![](nist-4score-prompts.gif)Figure 1: NIST Four Score Chart with different small dataset from the Canary Prompting datasets.


