[13/200] Running: What is the function of layer normalization in deep networks...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Review the implementation of layer normalization by looking up its code in PyTorch. What are some possible ways of implementing layer normalization that do not require you to pre-calculate the mean and variance in a pass? Consider the design choices that make it preferrable to batch normalization for RNNs.

1 Answers

Layer normalization helps in the faster learning of the model. As we know from deep learning fundamentals, normalization helps bring the variance down and helps in faster learning.
Batch Normalization works well when the input data is structured and are in batches. When the input data is not structured and is random or sequential, as in LSTMs, Batch Normalization doesn’t work well for obvious reasons. At that time, Layer Normalization works well. LN can normalize the entire layer without any dependency of the input dimensions.
During forward propagation, the activations per every input sample are normalized by calculating mean and variance over all the input samples per concealed layer. This is done by passing the input x through a fully connected layer.
An example code on LN is as follows:

import torch

import torch.nn as nn
class LayerNorm(nn.Module):
def __init__(self, features, eps = 1e-6):
super(LayerNorm, self).__init__()

gamma = nn.Parameter(torch.ones(features))

beta = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(features))
self.gamma = gamma

self.beta = beta

self.eps = eps
def forward(self, x):
mean = x.mean(-1, keepdim = True)

std = x.std(-1, keepdim = True)
norm_x = (x - mean) / (std + self.eps)

x = self.gamma * norm_x + self.beta
return x

