[15/200] Running: What is quantization and how does it improve inference perfo...

🧠 LLM Response:


Quantization is a technique in machine learning that lowers the precision of the machine learning model networks. By lowering the precision, the machine learning model achieves performance benefits and lower memory requirements.

Typically, machine learning models would be trained using float32 as 32 bit floating-point numbers, with 1 bit allocated for sign, 8 for exponent, and 23 bits set aside to store the number’s fractional part. This high precision is great for training the models but not ideal from a performance or memory perspective for inference.

Using float32 in machine learning models can yield performance issues due to bottlenecks that occur in the matrix multiplication process. It requires a lot of bandwidth resulting in slow computation. Also, float32 can struggle with any quantization of the other weights. It can result in incompatible values after quantization and produce large common loss. This means it needs a higher learning rate than if BFloat16 or other options were used.

During the training process, the weights may be stored at float32, but it is common to do calculations at lower precision to speed the process. Once training is complete, the weights are typically quantized (reduced precision) further for inference. E.g. Int8 ( 1 bit for sign, 3 bits for exponent, and 4 bits for the fraction which omits the decimal)

Half Precision Float (fp16) is a floating-point type that is 16 bits wide. It achieves 3x speed and 2x memory savings compared to fp32. Inference at full speed is preferred over training.

bfloat16 is also 16 bits wide, and fp16 is preferred for training models over bfloat16. And when quantizing (reducing precision), this format will reduce the impact precision has on the model. Typically, it is employed at training time to match softs performance.

## What are the benefits of Quantization?

Quantization (reducing precision) can be applied to machine learning models in two ways. Dynamic quantization (optional) and static quantization (required). There are pros and cons to implementing both strategies in a machine learning model.

Dynamic Ranges are assigned on the fly at runtime and during training. There is no range re-normalization. Data is used upon feature arrival. This method can reduce the code size by 50-70%.

Simple Advantages are smaller model size, faster evaluation/inference, reduced CPU/GPU utilization/load, etc. This comes at the expense of lower network accuracy sometimes. Some things can be done to mitigate the loss of accuracy such as using bfloat32 for computing during training time. Others include An adjustment to the loss function or performing floating optimization. The process of static quantization is shown below in Figure-1.

## Static Quantization

Static Quantization assigns ranges during training time. Data will be re-aligned to be on the same scale as the weights. This is a slower process but can result in higher performance inference.

### Improving static quantization

There are a few different ways to make static quantization even better:

- Good learning algorithms are key here. You will need to consider your target from the beginning. If you go training prematurely it can result in decreased performance. Keep in mind you will later need to convert to the destination format.
- Evaluate your learning algorithms with the target computing architecture, i.e., GPUs, CPUs, and even ASICs or custom processors. Evaluate the code in the target environment to know exactly how your network will perform.
- Even though the target data type might be Int8. Train your networks using FP32 or FP16. This allows you to fine-tune your network for higher accuracy.
- Make sure your training technique employs the same precision for other mathematical computations. e.g., If you reduce an image’s precision during training too early in the process it will need to be done again causing performance problems.

## In summary

Reducing the precision of a machine learning model has many benefits that range from improved performance and memory savings. Quantization is usually implemented using dynamic or static techniques. Dynamic assigns ranges to nodes on the fly, while static quantization happens offline. Good learning algorithms are vital in programs that use static methods because they have been trained to keep performance high.

