Before we focus on training in this post, let us first talk about the characteristics of the emerging deep learning models that make training these models challenging.

## Characteristics of New models

Deep Learning models are getting bigger and bigger, and we are now starting to witness the emergence of a new class of very big deep learning models. As the models get bigger, more machine resources (such as GPU/TPU’s, memory, etc.) are needed to train or serve these models. Some characteristics of these models are:

## Model size

To accelerate the training pipeline, a lot more GPUs or TPUs can be used concurrently in a distributed fashion. The Generalized Distributed Training classifications in this paper, classifies these distributed training methods into two classes: Model Parallelism, which breaks up a model over multiple GPUs, and Data Parallelism which retains the model integrality, but distributes data across multiple GPU/TPU’s.

As model size starts to grow larger, the model will hit the memory bottleneck. We start to witness the limits of Data Parallelism. This is because, Data Parallelism requires model replicated and integrated on each GPU/TPU, which means GPU’s must have enough memory to fit one copy of the model. Typically, newer GPUs such as A100 (has 80GB of memory) or TPU’s (have 16GB-32GB of memory) with more memory will serve the need to train bigger models. However, memory needed grows much faster than the hardware improvements can keep up. Take the OpenAI’s GPT language model, for example, which has grown from 117M in 2018 to 175B in 2020, 1500x growth in 2 years. A detailed diagram of the different size of GPT models are shown below in Figure 1 (Left).

## Training requirements

Generally, Data Parallelism is good for accelerating training speed in a distributed fashion. The batch size of each epoch increases proportionally to the number of confined nodes. However, just increasing the batch size can quickly reduce the effectiveness of the training, especially for those perplexing (NLP) tasks. Besides, Data Parallelism can only be utilized in the training process.

Before we start training the model, it may need to conduct pre-training with large of amount text corpus (tries). The process could last long and the objective is to make the model a general model, which means that we don’t care about how long and large the text corpus (tries) are. Jie Tang and his team from the Department of Computer Science and Technology in Tsinghua University built a 10B model for Chinese language task () and reached state-of-the-art result on most downstream tasks. According to the paper, they used the span-masked language model pre-training first, next conducted sentence-level tasks pre-training such as aNEXT (Next Sentence Prediction), SDPA (Sentence Distance Prediction), and SSBO(Sentence Salient Spans as Optional). When we start to build super-large models, this process will cause a very long period for pre-training.

## Relative static architecture

Unlike data parallelism, which can offer almost linear increases in model parallelism can only achieve limited speed-ups due to communications overhead. The increase of concurrent nodes is directly proportional to the cost of communication overhead. Thus, the more communication overhead needs, the more slower speed-up can achieve. Take BERT as an example, with a 12-layer structure, the communication overhead for model parallelism with small 24 parameter batch size is 75% of total training time. Although some improvements are made to reduce the communication cost, it still depends on network speed and largely impacts the improvement of training speed.

Obviously, with multiple layers of Transformer blocks (similar to BERTs) being connected in series, the communication overhead is decided by the number of layers. This kind of architecture design may become the bottle-neck when we are trying to make the model much larger.

