The HF transformers library contains models for high frequency transformers based on coupled inductors. The main difference between the standard Simscape™ Electrical™ Specialized Power Systems transformer models and the HF Transformers library models is that the HF Transformers library transformers are based on an inductive coupling coefficient k. The HF Transformers models use the common representation of the high-frequency transformers found in the literature. When creating a coupled inductor inside the Electric Drives Library, it is essential to express the leakage inductance and the magnetizing inductance in the same units.

Describe the typical applications that use the HF Transformers library.

The HF transformers library components represent an accurate electrical model of a real HF transformer which can be used for transformer sizing purposes, or to compare simulation results with results from vendor specifications or from lab measurements. The HF transformers component models can be included in a variety of applications that include DC to DC converters, Uninterruptible Power Supply (UPS), bi-directional AC to DC power supplies, and renewable systems.

What kind of studies can be performed with the HF Transformers library?

The HF Transformers library is a part of the Specialized Power Systems software, which is built on top of the Simscape environment and therefore takes advantage of its capabilities. The Special Power Systems HF Transformers library can be used in combination with other Specialized Power Systems components to create complex and realistic designs that deliver a variety of analyses such as, system level control, protection, system dynamics, and saturation behaviors in the high frequency transformers.

HF Transformers are available in two variants: regular inductive coupling and ideal inductive coupling. An ideal inductive coupling model assumes the transformer is lossless. There is no hysteresis. The regular inductive coupling model contains all losses and nonlinearities and may be applicable to ferrite transformers.

Illustrate the difference of modeling the control of the three-phase inverter.

Comparator based PWM control and Space Vector PWM are created using the same components inside Simulink®. The difference is in the way the reference signals are applied to the IGBT models, and the way the signal summation and comparator operations are performed.

When the system needs to adapt to changes in the power demand or the power available, is there a model that is better suited than another one?

The power conversion models of the Simscape Electrical Specialized Power Systems are composed of detailed electrical and magnetic representations of power components such as PWM-controlled inverters and rectifiers and a variety of transformers, such as three-phase, three-limb transformers and three-phase delta-delta transformers. The control designs are created using Simulink blocks instead of Specialized Power Systems components.

What does the back-to-back converter model consist of?

Back-to-back converters are composed of blocks from the Simscape Electrical Specialized Power Systems library. In this figure you can see one rectifier circuit, one inverter circuit and one DC-link. The DC-link is a simple capacitor with some parasitic resistance and inductance. The models of the power semiconductors can be linearized, detailed, turn-off, turn-on, detailed, linearized, averaged, or ideal.

What kinds of transformer models are available with the High Frequency Transformer library?

As a part of the HF Transformers library, two-port and three-port transformers with linear and nonlinear cores are available. The linear transformer models assume the transformer has a linear inductance profile. The nonlinear HF transformer models have a nonlinear inductance profile and consist of a hysteresis model and a nonlinear eddy current model (winding models), with all the losses present. Eddy current losses are computationally very expensive normally not feasible. The linear model is a reasonable tradeoff between computational cost and accuracy for many transformers.

What are the advantages of using a linear transformer model over a nonlinear transformer model?

Using a three-port coupling model reduces the number of transformers in the circuit because a three-port transformer can act as a regular three-phase transformer, but can also provide a connection to a load at a mid-tap. Linear models are computationally efficient with reasonable accuracy for many transformers.

