## Intuition

People often visualize intermediate layers of attention-based neural networks by treating the attention weights as pixel intensity or by plotting their saliency maps (e.g. “the model looks at where you thought it would look,” attention maps resemble human hand-coded segmentations, etc.). Sometimes this is useful and meaningful, but in many cases it’s more an understandable illusion than it is a truthful description of what actually went on (i.e. Aha! I knew curly brackets were broken. 😉).

It is difficult to separate the explainable from the spurious, because of the discrepancy between the image that was input into the network (does vector arithmetic happen linearly in our visual systems?) and what was important to finding the answer to a question, either by the network or by a person. Humans can construct narrative explanations for how they arrived at a task outcome [Dreyfus and Dreyfus, 1986, 553]. Neurons in the brain do pattern completion, matching two images that are transformations of each other, but we have trouble explaining how they do it without saying that “it’s a system of meaningful components” (e.g. David Marr [1976], Pylyshyn & Kosslyn [1993]).

That all amounts to the messy soup of analytical (symbols doing something) and synthetic (fair tests) approaches, and it does not permit us to distinguish facts from beliefs about neural network attention.

This paper contains an amusing footnote:

> …It could appear as though attention has moved to striped portions of an image where the network is already attending. But masks can be misleading.

There is a standard interpretation of attention heads on GHAA, a Visual Question Answering (VQA) task, that holds they are isolating the meaningful parts of the image (in the perspective of a subjective, human viewer), but in reality a single attention head causes contextually useful bottom-up facilitation and inhibition in several ways, and some of them are even eye-movement-reminiscent. (More on the “contextually meaningful” bit below).

## The Study

Two researchers at Google [Michael Ahuja and Oleksii Kuchaiev] publish extremely detailed experimental analysis of the attention heads employed by the Google Neural Machine Translation system to track down patterns in their behavior, specifically aimed at undercutting the standard interpretation of functional roles of GNMT attention heads in decomposing the source sentence into lexical constituents.

What they observe is a more complicated aggregation of functional roles assigned to attention heads, as they come together in a team (sometimes one takes charge; “spatially-distributed attention heads”).

The GNMT architecture is an encoder/decoder-style architecture with 8× 512-dimensional attention heads. These experiments were done over between 4 and 10 encoder layers and 8 decoder layers.

Ahuja, Michael, and Oleksii Kuchaiev. “What Does BERT Look At: An Analysis of BERT’s Attention.” Talk at NLP (Language and Time). March 30, 2019.

Once those scores are produced, three in-practice mechanisms for combining them result in distinct functional roles for the heads:

1. (In particular frames of video,) some attention to objects are contextually meaningful. That is, they reason with objects that emphasize space and time, and have no meaningful part for identifying the content of the image. To be concrete about examples:
    - Several images have attention heads dedicated to seas and sky, which are consistently in the upper reaches of the image.
    - Helicophoslovakia (Michael’s joke, not my journalism) has no long-range navigation autopilot guidance, so the camera pans up and back once in a while to show the car’s position relative to its destination.
    - Attention heads that localize a person of interest in the image, making a smooth minimum search over the rows and columns of the image to locate the subject’s face. (This is, of course, much like human search of a computer desktop.)
2. As the model stacks these object-based attention scores, they can change quite a bit between two related inputs (e.g. variations in the way that the question was asked).
