Purpose of the tokenizer's vocabulary

Tokens are not completely interchangeable, so a tokenizer needs to be told which types it should use. We will define a class for each type of token that we will require.

A Tokenizer with vocabulary will instantiate a vocabulary registry class that will be built, or have already been built in order to register all the types of tokens expected to be encountered. All newly created token objects will be registered by adding them to the vocabulary registry as a new, unique object.

The tokenizer will also populate a symbol table, which will be used to represent the tokens comprising the operands and operators for each statement. Ensure that vocabulary is kept to a minimum.

The vocabulary will contain every token type. The symbol table will use a lookup table made up of a pre-built set of token objects registered to a symbol table entry (pair) in a Key/Value collection.

The Vocabulary

The tokenizer vocabulary will use a method to register every token that we decide to use.

We can build a token string that will be complicated. A tokenizer has to parse everything that it is given properly or it will fail and terminate.

Together, the vocabulary registry and the symbol table enforce strict control of reference data used to recognize tokens provided to the tokenizer.

The vocabulary method returns the vocabulary registry for use in the tokenizer.

### Key Issues

#### The symbol table Key/Value pair collection

Taking care to build the tokenizer vocabulary lowers the cost of ownership. It makes the scope of use bigger.

Why is it important to build the tokenizer vocabulary?

This is necessary to ensure effective use of the tokenizer vocabulary as a plug-in. It allows the execution unit (interpreter) more control over the tokenizer.
