Mixture of experts architecture break the entire input space into partitions which are handled by the group of experts, which are themselves neural networks. The final output layer is provided as the gated linear combination of the individual outputs of the experts.

MIXED-DNN with 10 nodes in the hidden layer

All the nodes in a single layer receive same set of inputs of a DNN which are constrained to do the best mapping governed by a single function deffined by linearly combining prespective mappings . These maps actually handle totally different situations and thus it would be better to rearrange the mappings into groups, each handling different sort of situation and allowing the nodes belonging to a particular expert to collaborate with in a group. A gating network would then study the input pattern and activate a particular expert according to it.

Mixtures of experts is the terminology given to a specific neural network architecture. It is a flexible way for spitting the task into several regions of the input space, where each region is a network of its own. As illustrated in the figure, the Mixture of Expert Network consists of three main components,

i) A set of inputs *x* in the input layer
ii) A group of so-celled expert networks, which are really just regular neural networks.
iii) A gating network (a different neural network) whose task is to activate a particular expert to work on the problem.

Each expert is assigned a prior where gating network generate weights to average predictions from all the independently trained experts based on those prior outputs at the final output layer. This final layer may be a softmax step in the context of classification.

In the nutshell, a neural network consists of nodes that are of two types – input nodes and hidden layer nodes. All the nodes in a single layer receive same set of inputs which are constrained to do the best mapping governed by a single function defined by linearly combining perspective mappings. These maps actually handle totally different situations, and thus it would be better to rearrange the mappings into groups, each handling different sort of situations and allowing the nodes belonging to a particular expert to collaborate with in a group.

The figure above represents MoE (Mixture of Experts network).There may be any number of hidden layers, the last consisting of several output units `y_k`. The remaining variables, contain weights, as well as the offset terms, must be initialized; learning the weights (and any other the variables) appropriately will constitute the network training process and then the weights found can be applied to new input sets. MoE allows the outputs from several expert networks and combines them using a Gated Linear Network (for ex. a single layer feedforward network with linear activation function) consisting of one hidden node for each expert network. So Gated Network acts as a non-linear gate which gives weights to expert’s outputs forming a linear weighted sum leading to improved modeling.

- The neural network architecture, Mixture of Experts (MoE) and Hierarchical Mixture of Experts (HMoE) are two complex neural network which involves of parallel sub-models (`1...k`) that working together to produce a prediction.
- MoE is also known as Routing, as the expert outputs `t_1`, `t_2` ... `t_k` are routed to the final layer through a softmax layer that decides the output to the input. So basically the experts work like feature extraction, and the softmax layer select the “best” expert output to predict the target (YO).
- In MoE/HMoE, latent variables are treated as missing data. The Expectation Maximization (EM) algorithm may then be applied to determine Maximum Likelihood Estimates (MLE) of the parameters.
- Gating Networks in Neural Network Template: Main article: Self-Organizing Neural Networks (SONS) A gating network uses a non-linear function like a soft max to determine the input and output mapping from the expert neural networks. It also uses a layer of hidden neurons that implement a non-linear function.
