Vectors are your "data" and they're stored in a vector database, and help with vector-text retrieval.

LLMs (large language models) cannot remember things, so they can't remember their prompts. They can only use a context window of about 8000 tokens.

Enter the vector database.

A vector database stores vector embeddings retrieved from LLMs. It also stores the data that generated the embeddings.

The vector database takes queries and finds vector embeddings that you have stored in it. These embeddings have the text data associated with them. This is then fed back to the LLM in the form of a prompt along with the query. Alternatively, you can retrieve and use the data directly from the DB since a vector database can be used as a regular database. But the LLM can be used to combine other data with the results alongside performing other tasks.

You can then connect your vector database with your application. You can use your vector database to store and explore your query results.

