[43/200] Running: Describe how you would use RAG (Retrieval Augmented Generati...

🧠 LLM Response:


In Deng et al. (2021) the authors propose a procedure by which a natural language processing model could gain lot’s of ground truth text from sources like Wikipedia, without having the cost of creating and maintaining a first party datasource. This procedure, called as a retrieval algorithm, is one of four components to the RAG procedure. The other three components include Generation, Fusion and training algorithms. As a result, the RAG algorithm combines both a traditional text completions algorithm like BERT or GPT-x with the retrieval algorithm. In the following paragraph, I will describe each of these components in order.

The retrieval algorithm is given some fixed representation of the query and uses a model that maps representations of queries to a collection of texts and produces a score of whether these texts are relevant. Texts that are above a threshold score are said to be retrieved and are now available to the text generation algorithm. When there is no representation of the query available from previous sources the current query is converted into a vector and a retrieval algorithm will done on this transformed query representation.

The text generation algorithm is given the query, the original document and the retrieved results. The text generation algorithm produces a new body of text given the starting text, any end of sequence text and the retrieved results. The final component is the training algorithm.

The training algorithm places a text encoding model before the retrieval model, and then the output from this model is then the output for both the original text and retrieval results. The text encoding model when trained on a fusion algorithm will attempt to encode the results into some latent representation that combines the two models into the original text and the illustrates it more explicitely so that the text encoder can learn a strong representation.

After training is complete the original text is fed into the text generation algorithm with the results of the union of the latent representation of the text into the text encoder and the latent representation of the query generated by the retrieval algorithm as inputs. This allows the whole system to use results from a query retrieval system to augment a text generation algorithm, and hence the name retrieval augmented generation. Such a system with open source components could yield large impacts as the user base continues to grow. For instance, an explicit category of questions could be proposed like, “Can you think of any real world applications of artificial intelligence in climate change mitigation?”. This would then enable the retrieval algorithm to go and search forums such as Reddit for common uses, enabling a resource to be presented to the end user with consolidated information that is a step beyond searching on open engines like Google or Bing as this could yield more in depth answers.
[44/200] Running: What is streaming inference and how is it used?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Streaming inference, also known as online inference, is a technique used when inferencing large models that take up too much memory. An example of a situation involving streaming inference is when you want to perform image feature extraction on an image. Let's say the image is a 1000x1000 pixel color image. This encodes for 1,000,000 Pixels with 3 channels each, for a total of 3,000,000 feature values. If the model involves converting the image to a 20x20x3 matrix of neurons (where every 50x50 pixels is too one neuron each for the 3 channels), this would represent 1200 neurons. Each of the feature values could be linked to any one or multiple neurons, forming a giant graph.

So, how could you possibly encode such a model in software to make it easy and quick to add a new image for feature extraction? You could encode this matrix in memory; but, it would use up too much memory for no speed advantage, and likely would not fit in GPU memory either. Instead you can make use of streaming inference. In this method, you stream neurons to the GPU in batches, because the GPU has no way of knowing which of the 1200 neurons being loaded into memory will be required for inferencing the next feature value. After running the batch through the GPU, you can then repeat with the next batch until all neurons have been processed.

How can a TensorRT engine be configured to support streaming inference?

In order to enable streaming inference, your TensorRT engine needs to be built using the following builder configuration flag:

```
`builderConfig->setFlag(BuilderFlag::kFP16);`
```

The above flag causes the builder to create an FP16 engine instead of the default FP32 engine, the Tensor Core enabled engine can have up to 10,000 inputs and 10,000 outputs compared to the FP32 engine which can only have 1024 inputs and outputs.

This implies that a network definition which previously could not be declared due to large number of inputs/outputs, can be declared and have a Tensor Core enabled engine built from it.
All engines, regardless of FP32/FP16 mode, can use the ITensor interface to import the network and perform inferencing. So, if you already have your code in place, you won't need to modify it much for streaming inference, unless you were previously using a network binding, or a direct TensorIndex myself.

The ITensor defines a method accept() which accepts an ICudaEngine in the engine parameter on the CPU side and returns an ITensor on the GPU. This same ITensor interface is then used to run the inference and return the output results on the CPU. Inside the accept() method, you should do something similar to this:

```
`/* STEP 1: Run this once per TensorRT stream ID to upload`
 `the input and output pointers. (Their values for all`
 `streams can be the same) */`
`buffer->setOutputs( networkNumOutputs, 0, static_cast<int32_t>`
 `(numStreams) );`
`/* STEP 2: Run this inside the per-frame loop that is`
 `accepting the whole frame */`
`buffer->run();`
```

In this code, you will notice that there is a buffer object. In order for STreaming Inference to work you will need to inherit from IComputeStreamBuffer. This object should keep track of the IDs provided by:

```
`uint32_t streamID1 = hostEngine->getNumStreams();`
`uint32_t streamID2 = hostEngine->getNumStreams();`
```

These IDs can be used later in the accept() method, to identify the output buffers in call to IComputeEngine::setOutputs(). This in turn invokes IComputeEngine::setOutput(), which then triggers a call to the IComputeStreamBuffer::release() method.

Note that while a UPNet model could be instantiated with 10,000 inputs and outputs, this does not imply you can stream all 10,000 inputs to a network at once. This is because the TensorRT streaming engine has a limitation of up to MaxOutputsPerStream inputs to
