🧠 LLM Response:


## Non-CANVAS games

The TimesToFrame table is NOT used by non-CANVAS games.

The timing loop used by non-CANVAS games seems to aim for 60Hz by doing this:

- Run the game loop every millisecond(!).
- Skip the step and render cycle if render_after is less than a particular value - probably relative to current system time, and probably corresponding to a 60Hz frame rate.
- Otherwise, run the step and render cycle and then set render_after to the next 1/60 second boundary.

This means that:

- Games run as fast as the computer allows.
- Performance is not tied to the frame rate - the game doesn't care if the display is 60Hz, 50Hz or something else, the game will always try to step at 60Hz.

## CANVAS-based games

The Opposing Force engine uses the TimesToFrame table to determine the amount of delay to insert between cycles.

Each interaction step is determined to take 1/60 of a second (or 1/50 of a second) and the TimeToFrame table is used to convert that into the amount of delay to introduce in real time. Note: [HPE] the syntax of the table seems to imply that entries are specified in terms of ticks-per-frame rather than frames-per-tick.

In comparison with non-CANVAS games:

- Panel response feels much more sluggish as control in non-CANVAS games is not delayed by the sleep - in non-CANVAS games, the sleep time is only used to ensure a fixed maximum frame rate, while in CANVAS games the same value directly affects the polling loop itself.
- It has already been suggested that moving objects in [HPE] and [HPS] are almost too slow to handle.
