> - Each individual character is predicted by a small transformer and we use two ways of obtaining these:
>     - we use nearest neighbor retrieval from a matrix of characters that we call Key Value Cache (KV-cache). Each character is a lookup table entry and all entries combined form the KV-cache.
>     - we use the decoder to directly predict a fixed number of characters (usually 16 at the same time).
> - The speech encoder can also output task-specific embeddings which can be used together (multi-modal) with output tokens. Alternatively, you can directly output SVC embeddings.
> - For whole-word prediction, we store full words instead of individual characters in the KV-cache which reduces memory use (if we assume an average of six characters per word) by a significant margin (roughly five times as large depending on vocabulary size).
> - Our RNN-T loss uses a fully connected layer for classification instead of a simple softmax layer in order to utilize KV-cache lookups for faster inference.

