[48/200] Running: Describe token-by-token generation and its challenges....

🧠 LLM Response:
 How does subword regularization address these challenges?

For a language model to generate text token-by-token, it must decide at each step how to split words. For instance, the model may choose a split such as {{< /code>but it was partially full . }} on the first turn, then {{< /code>but it #was# partially #full # .}} on the second turn. However, the model states that the compound word “itwas” is not included in its vocabulary, hence its output isn’t realistic.

The significance of this problem cannot be understated given the enormous variety of writing conventions that are used on the internet. The same individual may spell the same term in a number of different ways, depending on the circumstances. A decent language model must be able to work with all spellings.

Subword regularization attempts to handle this issue by feeding many different pieces at the model’s entry on each loop. Because the model can see different splits of a token along the same sentence, the probability of it generates a word that isn’t in its vocabulary is significantly reduced.

For instance, using subword regularization is used, the model will see the second split that was remarked as unrealistic before. Not only has the number of bits of text the model can see increased, but so has the number of valid word splits.

The decision of which pieces to give the model as input is made using a type of stochastic process known as dynamic programming. The approach is designed with subword segmenters such as Byte-Pair-Encoding or subword loss and exponential word frequency factors in mind, as each of these encoders may efficiently yield reasonable word splits thanks to their inherent characteristics.
