[54/200] Running: Describe how memory-efficient attention works....

🧠 LLM Response:


Sparse Transformer [1] improved the memory requirement from $O({n}_{s}{n}_{s})$ to $O({n}_{s}k)$, where $k={n}_{c}n+{n}_{s}$.

- ${n}_{s}$: sequence length
- ${n}_{c}$: number of attention heads
- $n$: number of buckets per head

RFA [2] reduced asymptotic memory complexity from $O({n}_{s}{n}_{s})$ to $O({n}_{s}{n}_{r})$.

- ${n}_{s}$ is the same as above.
- ${n}_{r}$: the number of neurons in the Random Feature (RF) layers.

In comparison, the expansion rate ${n}_{r}$ is constant to the input length ${n}_{s}$. Hence, RFA is arguably more memory-efficient than Sparse Transformer.

However, the results in Context Dependent Representation Learning [3] suggested that random features lose to full attention or factorized attention most of the time. The full attention is theoretically the optimal and hence the RFA is suboptimal.

As long as full or factorized attention fits in the hardware, it is a better method over RF.
