Supervised Fine-tuning (SFT) is a popular machine learning technique used to tweak pre-trained models. It is commonly applied to large language models (LLMs), such as GPT-3 and GPT-4, to enhance their performance on specific tasks. With SFT, developers can refine and customize their model’s behavior, making it more effective for their intended use.

In this article, we will explore the technical aspects of supervised fine-tuning, the benefits it offers, and provides an easy guide on how to fine-tune LLMs for personalized performance. Read on to learn more about this cutting-edge technique and discover how you can harness its power in your machine learning projects.

## What is fine-tuning?

- Fine-tuning is one of two ways to train LLMs. It involves optimizing a pretrained model for a specific domain, such as cybersecurity or finance, to improve its performance in that area.
- In fine-tuning, only a few layers of the model are adjusted while the rest remain unchanged. This makes the model leaner and easier to execute, since only the specific sections of the dataset are used to train the model. As a result, fine-tuned models can perform more efficiently than pretrained models, although they may sometimes lose some general knowledge.
- With fine-tuning, users can create no-code models for specific tasks such as sales or support by incorporating real-world data without the need for specialized language experts to label huge datasets. By feeding in live customer data, the model can learn from and be fine-tuned to improve its ability to respond to customer inquiries in the desired way.

## What is Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)?

- Supervised fine-tuning (SFT) involves tweaking a pre-trained model to enhance its language capabilities by training it on labeled datasets. This approach guides the model’s performance in a specific direction and improves it on a specific task or dataset.
- In supervised learning, the input data used to train the model is labeled or annotated. The pre-trained model is then fine-tuned using this labeled data to improve its performance on a specific task or dataset.
- SFT allows models to recognize structures and patterns more easily through alignment of their learning goals with labelled datasets, enabling them to mimic human behavior and generate text with greater fluency.
- Techniques like Supervised Fine-Tuning help to train a fine-tuned GPT model or any other large language model (LLM). At the heart of successful machine learning, SFT involves training a model to generate text that reads accurately according to the specifications laid out by its creators.

## What does Supervised Fine-Tuning do

Fine-tuning is a method used in large language models such as OpenAI’s ChatGPT or Google’s Bard to customize the model for a specific task. Fine-tuning involves using machine learning to optimize the model’s output based on a labeled dataset.

The dataset is annotated with information specific to the task, such as being funny, friendly, and correctly answering questions. By training the model on this dataset, it can learn to generate output that is tailored to the desired behavior.

Fine-tuning allows creators to personalize the behavior of the model to produce specific types of text and further train the model to perform at a high level of competence. Fine-tuning is used in various natural language processing tasks, such as sentiment analysis, named entity recognition, and language translation.

The goal of fine-tuning is to improve the model’s performance on a specific task by training it on a dataset that is relevant to that task. This is in contrast to pre-training, which involves training a model on a large and diverse dataset to learn general language representations that can be applicable to a wide range of tasks.

## How does Supervised Fine-Tuning work?

