## Causal attention mask (Definition)

> A causal attention mask is a computational concept used in Transformers to enforce causality in autoregressive language modeling.

Wait. Hold on. Causality + autoregressive?

Causality and autoregression at the first glance might feel natural in any AI literature or speech recognition.

In fact, they are.

So despite the AI scientific backgrounds that can also be found in the articles shared, let’s translate causality in terms of Transformers and causal attention mask.

Alright, so causality means sequentially incremental modeling. More specifically, instead of generating all the words of your next e-mail together, you might need to model them one at a time.

We cannot just generate some sentence and then re-model other words before, and that is what causality stands for. The same is true for projects working with signal data.

Where does the autoregressive part come in?

In autoregressive modeling, we assume that time-series values are dependent on their values at preceding times, e.g. at the previous time step, t-1.

Instead of assuming randomness, autoregressive models interpret data as a sum of previous inputs triggered by an unobserved error term. Therefore, autoregressive modeling is neither random nor chaotic, as some suggest.

So causality and autoregression are profoundly connected and can be broadly translated in terms of machine learning and attention models.

