[68/200] Running: What are fused kernels and why are they fast?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Kernel fusion refers to the technique of combining more than one small function into one larger function. Inside GPUs, the term is often used to describe the combining of two simple kernels into one slightly more complex kernel to improve performance.

But my kernels are already optimized!

Wrong! Well, almost certainly wrong. Even the simplest of cases can benefit from kernel fusion. See Appendix A for an experiment (and explanation!) demonstrating how it can be useful to fuse the random numbers kernels, including a simple generate random numbers kernel with a fill kernel. .

What needs to be considered when fusing kernels?

### Latency

One thing I didn’t consider was the time it takes to switch a kernel running on one block to start running on another block to hide the memory latency. It takes about 200 processor clock cycles to switch, and can affect performance of your fused kernels. We’ll revisit this concept at a later time, there’s more investigation to be done.

### Global Memory vs Shared Memory vs Texture vs Constant Memory

The type of memory used in most CUDA kernels is global memory, which has lower bandwidth and higher latency than shared memory (which is essentially the L1 cache). When we consider kernel fusion, we need to know the memory access latencies of the different memory types, but also keep in mind that the on-chip shared memory is a finite and valuable resource. One problem associated with kernel fusion is also the possibility that the fused kernel assumes the ability to share more shared memory and, in fact, it is the case. This must always be analysed as you eliminate more and more separate kernels.

### Are kernels or memory bound?

It is helpful to identify if a kernel is compute bound or memory bound prior to fusing. One way is to use the performance metrics mentioned above. If the memory latencies are not the biggest concern, the chances of improving performance with memory optimisations (such as kernel fusion) are less likely.

That’s it for now! In the next post, I’ll take a look at HOW to fuse kernels.

