🧠 LLM Response:


## Overview

The illustration below shows the typical process of model serving using vLLM. First, Dockerfiles of frontend (`app`), backend (`model-serving`) and worker (`llama2_worker`) should be built. The instructions for building these Dockerfile can be found at Build Dockerfiles.

Next, `vllm.conf` (which is optional) and `llama2_worker.yaml` (which is mandatory) must be configured. `vllm.conf` is a configuration file required for model serving, while `llama2_worker.yaml` is a configuration file for `llama2_worker`. Please see Configure Configuration Files for more details on how to configure them.

The above `vllm.conf` and `llama2_worker.yaml` must be stored in a location accessible to both backend and workers. In this example, they are stored in `s3://kube-system/vllm-quickstart`.

Finally, once the above preparations are completed, the application can be launched with the command `helm install`.

## Walkthrough

The following section demonstrate the above flow in more detail. It assumes the configuration file `llama2_worker.example.yaml` is located at `./llama2_worker.example.yaml`, and the modified configuration files are stored at `s3://kube-system/vllm-quickstart` such that the backend and worker can access them.

### Build Dockerfiles

First, you need to build `llama2_worker` using `llama/llama:1.1.0-sf001-docker` and store it in your Dockerhub. Please refer to Build Worker Dockerfile to learn how to build Dockerfile for `llama2_worker`.

```bash
cd examples/llama2/Dockerfile_examples/based_on_llama1.1.0

# Modify the image field in ./Dockerfile_worker.yaml to the right location.
# Change ./llama2_worker.example.yaml as well.
# Also, don't forget to change ./vllm.conf's vllm_address.
helm -n vllm install quickstart \
    charts/llama2/ \
    -f ./llama2_worker.example.yaml \
    -f ./vllm.conf \
    --set "app.mode=server" \
    --set "app.image=$IMAGE_SEARCH/try123456:llama2_worker" \
    --set "app.replicaCount=1" \
    --set "app.sessionNum=1" \
    --set "app.pvcVolumeHandle.setupPersistentVolume=false" \
    --set "app.logLevel=LOG_DEBUG"

cd examples/llama2/Dockerfile_examples/worker_llama2_gpu/

# Modify the image field in ./Dockerfile_worker.yaml to the right location.
# Change ./llama2_worker.example.yaml as well.
# Also, don't forget to change ./vllm.conf's vllm_address.
helm -n vllm install quickstart \
    charts/llama2/ \
    -f ./llama2_worker.example.yaml \
    -f ./vllm.conf \
    --set "app.mode=server" \
    --set "app.image=$IMAGE_AZUR:llama_gptq_v1.1.0_gpu_batch_gptq6bit_v100" \
    --set "app.replicaCount=4" \
    --set "app.sessionNum=1" \
    --set "app.logLevel=LOG_DEBUG"
```

Take note that `app.mode` is set to `server`, `app.replicaCount` is set to `4`, and `app.sessionNum` is set to `1`. By adjusting the `app.replicaCount` to a value larger than 1, model serving can be done using multiple workers, and the `--set "app.logLevel=LOG_DEBUG"` portion of the command assists in providing detailed logs that are helpful in debugging issues. Once the `helm` installation phase is completed, you can test the serving
