## Will ChatGPT-like AIs Defeat Us at Science?

APS News March 2023

“Can scientists also make an artificial intelligence system that would beat us at science?

Probably not, but definitely not any time soon. Emerging large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT and Google's Bard can indeed sling meaningless, wildly offensive, or hilarious responses around. But at the end of the day, this is not the same as making scientific headway toward a better understanding of the world around us. Hence, comparing Goodhartian metrics for human-written papers with those for LLM-generated ones is an incoherent and ill-advised exercise. …”

## Why AI-generated papers could be a boon for science

AAAS Science March 2023

“As large language models (LLMs) such as GPT-3 and ChatGPT take the world by storm, a worrisome trend has emerged. People have begun to use these generative algorithms to write papers intended for publication. On preprint servers, guidelines are already in place to prevent uncritical publication of such papers. However, there are interesting opportunities hidden behind the threat of LLM-generated science. … LLMs can also identify relevant concept attributes that researchers in the scientific community may have overlooked. … LLM technologies have just as much potential to revolutionize science as they do to degrade it. If LLMs are used intentionally—to bias research toward important attributes, for instance—they may rival (and potentially help) humans in our scientific endeavor. We should welcome the opportunities promised by LLMs while keeping a watchful eye on their potential traps.”

## Using ChatGPT to improve ChatGPT: Designing Instructions for Hard Conversations

Arxiv, February 2023. Submitted to the 30th International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence.

“In certain scenarios, we might prefer ChatGPT to act primarily from a rational point of view, not from a very human and emotional one, but its behaviors are not yet customizable to our own preference. In this work, with only a few short conversations utilizing designed instruction and thought process requests, we directly improve its reliability in hard conversations, achieving 1801.17% improvement in a first 30 questions of truthful QA set and 47.70% improvement in an AI Alignment test set in the first view. … Given ChatGPT's proven ability to achieve SOTA results on many benchmarking tasks even with different types of its training objectives, ChatGPT's behavior and decisions can be largely influenced just by the instruction. In contrast to generative prompting, these simple instructions puts almost no constraint on expressing exact possibilities of outputs. Instead, we give guidance on thought process emotion, which can significantly affect ChatGPT's final chat content. … Most interestingly, by including requests like "Reason in simple steps", we greatly improved ChatGPT's long-term direction. A simple "Yeah" or "Ok" toggle would also potentially make a huge difference in controlling certain conversation turns. If ChatGPT, like these apps, can achieve such a toggle, they will be surprisingly helpful in designing certain instructive conversation turns and greatly simplify managing ChatGPT optimization progress. …”

