🧠 LLM Response:
 In this work, we explore this question using GPT-2 and RoBERTa fine-tuned on the MTurked Rotten Tomatoes review dataset (Bittel et al., 2021), where the model only has access to context information to guess target sentiment, which remains hidded. In the baseline setting (bottom left), the model has access to the prompt (context) and we vary the temperature, and we observe an expected saturation effect, where having extreme temperatures distorts the predicted words, and around 1.0 yields the best predictions but is still imperfect, yielding estimated scores substantially different from the ground truth. How can we achieve a better match with the ground truth? Our result suggests that humans often suspect that something has gone wrong with the model when it produces literal nonsense, and thus we adjust the model prediction by doing a re-sample to get the most adjacent word that is understandable and intelligible. Interestingly, we observe that the difference between the ground truth and the prediction decreases steadily across temperatures as temperature increases, in other words, as the model prediction gets more difficult to understand, the guesses towards the target sentiment become more dependent on the temperature of the predictions. This may be an artifact of the model not being able to use context well, and relies too much on the temperature for rating the sentiment of a given snippet.

