In GPT-3-vs-humans studies, we've seen stupidly-skilled journalism. Researchers fooled professional fact-checkers using GPT-3.
We're officially in an age where text generators are spooky good.
As far as I understand, the most-studied form of text generation is pure generation, where you feed an image (or “prompt”) to a language model and ask it to write a text caption. But in order to craft something truly useful to people, we'll need models that can get good at limited, “constrained” text generation.
One underrated feature is the model’s ability to generate on-demand, contextually-aware descriptions. How well can GPT-3 describe variations of a prompt you give it?
For example, if you feed it an empty world…
"What is the texture of this empty unfurnished white room?"
...it can go crazy with color and creativity like so...
"Padded walls. Light blue walls, plush rugs, thickly padded carpets, and dark hardwood floorboards. No art, windows or creaky doors. Just a bed against the back wall, a lone chair beside it. Two ceiling fans, and a single skylight with a clear plastic window. No curtains. The room is completely dark except for what's radiating upward."
It's mind-blowing to watch a machine build a world from nothing, right? This ability will be foundational for a lot of fun applications, from automated Shakespearian dialogue to word policing.
But there's a big problem: Model hallucinations.
Garbage in, garbage out
Hallucinations, unfortunately, are rife in generated descriptions.
One of the best examples of this came out of the Imagen model's release. The "caption" one puts in is a strong contributing factor, as well as model choice. The image comes from the Imagen model's examples.

As you can see, "family wearing white" primes the model to generate a photo filled with faces. "Cartoon of pink and black unicorn winking", on the other hand, results in a gruesome, terrifying animal that looks like the dead dog from Antichrist (2009). Only 3 of 4 images are correct.
There have been dozens of posts on this since release; Deep Learning FTW! had the best summary of the situation:
"To my eyes it looks like some mode mismatch. It’s as if the model is hallucinating, the image it generates is relevant but only a fraction of it is relevant."
So once we remove all the various platform factors—model size, training data, etc.—what’s left… and how does it relate to translation? Well, it leaves us with a surprising similarity to the fine-grained hallucinations we see in BLEU, a metric commonly used to evaluate translation quality.
And we should be able to use this knowledge to build tests and tools to combat hallucinations.
Relation to translations?
English machine translation seems pretty good these days, right? It’s basically solved already!
Whether absolutely or relatively, I think this is a dangerous take. Translation is so, so far from solved. And there are many reasons why.
What you're reading right now, for example, is likely an English-centric, Google-centric perspective. This perspective overweights the one algorithm tackling six languages, differentiating between six character sets. Indeed, for a paper released 3-4 years ago (2019, 2020), Google’s NMT system was found to produce “the most fluent” output (and not—by any means—the most BLEU points). The paper cited four other previous models that each scored more valid BLEU results.
More importantly, current translation scores only give us pseudo-granular awareness of specific error types.
