[91/200] Running: How are fused attention layers implemented?...

🧠 LLM Response:

It is assumed that tests and training sets contain a unique label per level which denotes the information to be calculated. Consider the following example a standard machine translation (MT) sequence to sequence learning architecture. It uses fused attention layers at every decoder level. Overall, we have three levels of training data, one of them containing the inputs and the outputs of the input text pairs for the translations. The second one is computed from the first one, because it calculates the amount of attention the decoder pays on the inputs during training, on a token level. Normally there would be a float per output token per input token. With fused attention layers in place, these weightings are generated via fusing with the labels of another table. In this case, the weights table for the previous attention level (the training data) was chosen, so there is only a single weight calculated per output token. Other labels from different tables and levels can be used as well - see section on fusion tables for details. The third label is a double used to assess the probability of the correct token being predicted next. Which training data level is used here is defined in the tbstring file's "acceptedTokenList" directive's values. For the example of the CtrlGen.tbstring.txt training data setup, it then looks like this:

```
acc...train...sumA1...input-0...input-10...output-0...output-10...weights-0...weights-10...accept...accept-0...accept-10
```

Since the last part is 0-10, it means that 10 levels are used, the first 0 is not of interest since all tokens that can be used in the base layer are listed in the training data (input-0 and output-0). The 10 levels are actually four, since only four different values are used, 0, 3 and 6, which means layer 0, 3 and 6. More precisely, the setup involves 4 levels of which the label of the previous one is used to compute the current one since the keywords "fusionSumA1" and "fusionSumA3" are used:

```
acc...train...sumA1...input-0...input-10...output-0...output-10...weights-0...weights-10...accept-0...accept-3...accept-6
acc...train...sumA3...input-11...input-20...output-11...output-20...weights-11...weights-20...accept-11...accept-14...accept-17
acc...train...sumA1...input-21...input-30...output-21...output-30...weights-21...weights-30...accept-21...accept-24...accept-27
acc...train...sumA3...input-31...input-40...output-31...output-40...weights-31...weights-40...accept-31...accept-34...accept-37
```

In JTRepLite, one notices that for each level the weights of the previous attention layer are used:
Code:

```
com.sri.ai.util.matrix.DenseMatrix 100,100 float-array 2
   ...
   fusionSumA1 153.4174
   ...
   fusionSumA1 153.4174
   ...
```

And the next line the next attention weights are used (0 value for the first fused layer):
Code:

```
com.sri.ai.util.matrix.DenseMatrix 100,100 float-array 2
   ...
   fusionSumA1 0.0
   ...
   fusionSumA1 0.0
   ...
```

The calls to the fuse() function are done in Seq2SeqArticleCompletionPtr.java,

at the end of the functions in lines 1136, 1343, 1406 and 1508.

```
oMatrix.setIndex(weights, weightsForWeightType);
```

The fusion weights can be used for what is called "guiding a network during training". Fused attention layers do even more things! So I added another section detailing how fused attention layers are used during training to evaluate word probabilities
