Artifical neural networks (ANN) have been around for quite some time, but have again been a popular tool in machine learning since 2010, when Geoffrey Hinton and his team^{[1]} submitted a breakthrough paper. Thanks to the introduction of so-called deep models, which are ANN with many layers of neurons, several previously unsolvable problems could be attacked. In NLP, ANN are used to learn the meaning of words from large corpora and to retrieve them as high-dimensional representations or word embeddings.

One type of ANN are recurrent neural networks, which show very good performance for tasks that involve the processing of sequential data such as machine translation or text-to-speech-systems. Starting from the one-dimensional chain-like architecture, researchers have proposed enhancements to the initial model that empower such networks to handle even more difficult tasks. In this post, we will introduce the basics of recurrent neural networks and will then take a look at the sequence-to-sequence model.

### Recurrent neural networks

One of the earliest and most popular recurrent neural network setups is the Long Short-Term Memory Network (LSTM). Compared to standard single-layer feed forward networks and their multilayer generalization, recurrent neural networks allow feedback connections. This enables them to learn sequential, temporal relationships.

Figure 1: Comparison of feed-forward and recurrent neural network; the hidden layer h is fed back to the network in order to save information

The left part of Figure 1 depicts a network of the form

with parameters that are learned utilizing a backpropagation method. The output o is computed at each time step t based on the input vector x. Recurrent neural networks are capable of using information from previous points in time by introducing hidden layers [h] which allow to recurrently feed back information to the network as depicted on the right of Figure 1. This enables these models to learn long-range dependencies by propagating information backwards in time to all layers of the network.

The original LSTM model takes sequences of input data and learns long-range dependencies in these by using a so-called Gated Approach. In an LSTM there is a set of gates that determines the information flow within the cell such that the output is based on a filtering of its content. This is possible by calculating a function f based on information at t-1 , which decides how much of the output is being fed back to the network at the next time step t. In our example of text classification, f will compute a value between 0 and 1 depending on the importance of the information for the task at hand.

Now, what’s inside an LSTM cell? First, it can be formally written as a tuple (c, h) with a memory state c and a hidden state h. The question is how the information from one time step to another time step is being preserved. It turns out that the memory cell uses a combination of mechanisms for remembering and forgetting parts of the input pattern from earlier time steps. This is why the term Long Short-Term Memory was chosen for this class of neural networks.

Figure 2: The LSTM cell at work

Now, what do we see in Figure 2? It might look a bit complicated at first, but is actually quite easy to understand once being explained: x denotes the input vector at a time t. First, the input x as well as a hidden state h from the previous time t-1 are used to create input and forget gate activations and a and f using equation (1) and (2) below.

$a_t = \sigma(w_a [h_{t-1}, x_t]+b_a)$

$f_t = \sigma(w_f [h_{t-1}, x_t]+b_f)$

The following steps will then determine the values of and which denote the information that is added and removed from memory, respectively by:

$i_t = \tanh(w_i [h_{t-1}, x_t]+b_i)$

$o_t = \sigma(w_o [h_{t-1}, x_t]+b_o)$

