 Where a series of questions can be asked to narrow the focus for the teacher to assist a pupil in their learning, in turn securing greater progress. In this post, we have offered some examples for developing 5 key skills.

The post has been designed and researched with our friends over at School Organisation Services, the number one school development consultancy. A highly recommended and trusted company who we regularly wheel in for help, for example, with PRU provision. Not only this but they manage to hook their readers with some of the best headteacher blogs around.

1………………… 2…………………….. 3.

The three-part lesson structure is so firmly wedged into the national consciousness that it is now removed from evidence that it works; indeed, Pearson and Lodge (2014:46) describe it as becoming ‘addicted.’ The fact that it is difficult to find evidence to endorse such a practice does not mean that it does not work! How students progress in any subject area should determine the method that is used. Part of this issue is that it is taught to teachers and trainee teachers without a precise idea that this structure has been devised to enable students to make progress. So, let us start with three truths:

1. Children in our primary and secondary schools need to be motivated constantly to learn more.
2. Children love learning; no child says that they used to work hard, then gave up, but they now love to muck around.
3. Progressive techniques are out of fashion; our secondary school students had a far richer classroom experience through mixed-ability classes and flexible cross-phased timetables.

Decoding Strategies

So let’s look at the decoding of your questions. How can we repeat something like a direct question- for instance, does it always need to be affirmation/negation? A small addition such as oscillating, sequencing, suggesting, interpreting helps demarcate a response. We designed the below decoding strategies for this exact purpose.

Literacy Decoding

- Level 1– To listen actively to and support others
- Level 2/3– To make predictions about what might happen
- Level 4/5– To explain their views using concrete examples and references
- Level 6- To infer conclusions from what is known and what has been
- Level 7/8– To speculate and construct different possibilities

Numeracy Decoding

- Level 1/2– To select the mathematical tools to acquire a solution
- Level 3/4– To select the mathematics to acquire a solution
- Level 5/6– To select the correct process to acquire a solution
- Level 7/8– To solve problems by creating their own method.

ICT Decoding

- Level 1/2- Understanding instructions and solving common problems
- Level 3/4- Technical instructions, fault finding, adapting configuring functions.
- Level 5/6-Using advanced formula to specify or modify existing functions.
- Level 8/9-Developing or configuring new functions.

Social/emotional Decoding

- Level 1/2– To support and sustain successful group activity.
- Level 2/4 –To criticise and give feedback reflecting on individual’s strengths and weaknesses of peers
- Level 4/5– To show leadership skills in organising events.
- Level 4/5– To show initiative in the application of problem-solving.
- Level 6/7– To manage time for self-evaluation and direct planning.

Science Decoding

- Level 1/2- Knows key facts and recognise theories behind observations.
- Level 2/4– Identifying applications of scientific ideas in context and considers multiple solutions.
- Level 4/5– works well in groups and decides courses of action.
- Level 5/6– Follows successful reasoning in finding a solution.
- Level 7/8– can constructively criticise cause and effect.

It is a peculiarly interesting philosophy that action is worth more than words. That the teacher needs to be active, considering ways to help in a practical sense, rather than encouraging students to talk through their problems. A common thread in effective classrooms is that low-level discussions, preferable with the homework involved, would not disrupt the flow of the session, this needs to re-emphasised in terms of active learning for all pupils and teachers, whereas teacher talk does not have this requirement.

