[99/200] Running: How are model prompts structured in ChatML?...

🧠 LLM Response:

======

A ChatML prompt is made up of several parts, each of which serves a specific purpose:

String inference
----
String inference adds contextual information to the prompt. Most of the time, it is written from the model's point of view. Any system message passed to the model must also include a string inference command. This inference is not returned to the client.

A string inference command has the following structure:

```
ModelStringAction:  <!-- Your string data -->
```

Variables
----
Variables are predefined variables that the user must declare. To do this, use the following syntax:

```
model_variable_id: "command"
```

In this example, model_variable_id is the variable's identifier, and "command" is the command to be executed.

<aside>
⚠️ Be aware that chat models produce output in natural language format, so you should anticipate unexpected behaviors. A common issue is that models tend to include variable names in their answers.

</aside>

User input
----
User input supplies input data, which is usually in text format. When provided, this input is expected to be used in the response.

The user input command has the following syntax:

```
UserMessage: <User input goes here>
```

Specify the variable in the input field.

```
UserMessage: {model_variable_id}
```

System commands
----
A system command sends a direct instruction to the model to execute a certain action. System commands can take on a range of formats, but they typically begin with the word "system," followed by a colon and the command itself.

Here is the structure of a simple system command:

```
SystemCommand: command
```

This command instructs the model to execute the specified action.


