[109/200] Running: How to handle long sequences in generation?...

🧠 LLM Response:


# Abstract

Modern Transformers routinely handle inputs tens of thousands of tokens long during inference (i.e., for text generation), despite being trained on much shorter sequences. This mismatch carries the risk of hallucinated and ungrammatical text during generation, even as standard training objectives would suggest that shorter sequences are easier to fit. We examine the problem of long sequence generation and show that – despite no train-time exposure and limited fine-tuning – a simple caching mechanism allows models to generate coherent long text containing novel information. Qualitatively, mistakes in such generations are more grammatical than at shorter lengths. Our work opens new avenues for research into long sequence generation and how models of limited capacity can preserve and extend beyond the information they have been fed.

# Conclusion

Future work could also consider generating syntax trees rather than raw text, and gener- ating large input lengths either by regularizing inputs during training or choosing generation methods that allow the model to keep some information internal when the sequence it is generating exceeds the length it was trained on.
