Fault tolerance, the capability that one component can continue to function when an error occurs on another component, is implemented on computing systems at differing levels (high level, micro kernel, or hardware programmable interrupt controllers with machine check architecture) — either within software or as hardware features.

Computers can have memory and processor faults. Checkpoints are one way to enable system fault tolerance. A checkpoint removes all data from memory, rapidly catalogues it, and maintains its availability, keeping user programs intact during even something dire, like a power outage.

