[121/200] Running: What is speculative decoding and how does it help?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 That's the question I wanted answered when I ran across this article on High Scalability. Turns out there's not a lot written about it. What I did find is obscure and difficult to interpret. Decided to apply what I know of chip design and been reading about AI to see if I could dig up a decent explanation.

Since I was able to make sense of it, I thought I'd document what I learned.

Here's the take-away. Decoding is ugly and hard to do accurately. Speculating what is intended in a particular chunk of code is usually a bad idea. But these days computers are so fast use of guessing based on what was requested during decoding (ie branch prediction) is no longer unreasonable. Yes, incorrect guesses cost clock cycles. But with parallel decoding, you can easily make up the time spent on a "do-over."

## Decoding

For complex instructions, an instruction set architecture (ISA) pseudocode to assembly and assembly to machine code translation pipeline is complicated. Details vary by instruction. To speed up processing, the decoder looks for certain repeated patterns and routines it can use and pass to the next processor in the pipeline that will be executing the instruction.

As much work as chip designers put into this aspect of chip design, it's difficult to provide 100% coverage for all the instructions possible in a particular instruction set architecture. Hence the difficulty calculating properly has been likened to "micro-code spaghetti" (Hyperscale).

There's overhead incurred as well. On the cycle where decoding happens, the instructions we'll execute are decoded and the right sequence and all the right data passed along to the processor on the following clock cycle that is responsible for execution.

Afterward, the decoder would typically do nothing to speed up the pipeline. Hence pipeline utilization is less than optimal. It waits until the right instruction comes along. And recalculates what to pass to the next processor in the pipeline. Again overhead results.

## Speculative Decoding

In this case, instead of just sitting on the next clock cycle unused, what if the decoder was busy decoding the next possible instruction (remember the pseudocode and assembly to machine code translation pipeline) before it had the opportunity to look at the actual instruction issued?

On first look, it's a bad idea. But on closer examination, it makes a lot of sense. Users typically repeat their behavior. Errors in the decoding process are rare.  Why not guess the next instruction to be decoded using branch prediction based on what was decoded in the previous cycle.  Since we can cache the prior results somewhere in the chip, the decoder has a good idea what it should be working on even before it processes the instruction on the clock cycle in progress.

When the decoder recognizes it has made an incorrect guess, there's some rework to do. So incorrect guesses are just a waste of clock cycles. But with parallel decoders, the unutilized (during normal processing) decoder  is fired up and uses what it learns on  the prior clock cycle to continue the process of decoding the instruction on. Hence, there's really no lost time when an incorrect branch prediction occurs.

## Net Results

- After an error is recognized in a prior branch speculation, extra work is required to bring the non-speculative decoder up to speed.
- We are speculating, not the big deal. We know the set of possible instructions to decode based upon the instruction decoded on the prior clock cycle.
- After a branch miss there's an extra clock cycle or two spent determining the penalty, the processing diverges to what actually needs to be decoded, and various internal states have to be set appropriately.
- Cache makes the speculation guess accurate if the same instruction sequence is seen again.
- Branch prediction accuracy is good enough (Hyperscale) to make the overhead of handling two potential decodes most of the time (though not all) worth the extra energy.
