[139/200] Running: What are attention heads and how do they specialize?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Although there’s been a substantial amount of work on understanding the roles of individual units in multilayer perceptrons (MLPs), convolutional neural networks (CNNs), and even recurrent neural networks (RNNs), comparatively few papers have studied the attention heads in a popular model family known as transformers (Vaswani et al., 2017). Given the success of transformer models, they are worth analyzing in credit to improve our understanding and interpretability of deep networks. In “What Does BERT Look At? An Analysis of BERT’s Attention,” (Clark et al., 2019), the authors examine BERT’s attention heads. However, this post emphasizes their systematic finidings rather than the intricate details of analyzing every layer of the network.

# A Variety of Reasons to Analyze Attention Heads

Before delving into the results, consider some possible motivations for analyzing attention heads. At a minimum, knowing where individual attention heads focus could be useful for debugging downstream NLP tasks or even basic understanding of the models in question. Effectively debugging algorithms could be a crucial factor when deciding between two architectures to utilize. From the perspective of training, transformers can be considered a black box, and any knowledge that pulls the curtain back can illuminate current or future models. It might prove helpful to ablate attention heads when finetuning a general-purpose model for a specific task. Knowing the performance of individual attention heads on tasks other than natural language inference could help multi-task learning setups.

# Quantitative Definition of Important Words

The paper analyzes a specific set of attention heads within the trained BERT (BERT\textsubscript{BASE}) model. Because BERT\textsubscript{BASE} is a 12-layer model with 12 attention heads per layer, there are a total of 144 heads to analyze. Any mention of BERT within this article refers to this specific pretrained model. Because BERT\textsubscript{BASE} is a masked language model, it receives partial input sentences, attempts to predict the missing words, and receives a cross entropy loss. Clearly, some words in each sentence can be more important than others, but an algorithmic definition of important words is given, using the cross-entropy loss of individually masked words. First, the NLP task of natural language inference (NLI) is simply to determine whether a “HYPOTHESIS” is true (entailment), false (contradiction), or undetermined (neutral) based on a “PREMISE.” Simple examples of this task, as provided in the paper, are:

PREMISE: Two women are embracing while holding to go packages.

HYPOTHESIS: Two women are high-fiving. (NEUTRAL)

PREMISE: Someone is blowing up balloons with a bicycle pump.

HYPOTHESIS: Someone is getting ready for a party. (ENTAILMENT)

Unsurprisingly, BERT proves useful for this task when finetuned on the MultiNLI corpus. It shows an accuracy of 86.6% on the test set.

While analyzing which attention heads specialize in specific words, it’s not enough to analyze only the weights before the softmax layer in the multi-head mechanism. Even if an attention head places a large weight on a specific word, there’s no guarantee that the encoder representation of the hidden state is meaningful for that word. To overcome this, importance is measured through ablation instead. Ablating a word means choosing to ignore it and only keeping the other words in a given sentence. One can then calculate the loss that results from ignoring that individual word. Then the relative importance of a word within a sentence is determined by the difference between a sentence’s original loss and when that specific word is ablated. In the extreme case, removing the word with the highest gain first probably results in a larger increase in loss than ablating any other word in the sentence. The procedure continues until all words in the sentence have been ablated once. Thus, each word is classified based on its rank in the most important set. The entire process can be run over an NLI test set and averaged to obtain mean ranks for an entire corpus. This approach suggests an importance-ranked view of BERT’s attention heads for the NLP task of natural language inference.

# What Attention Heads Pay Attention To

