[143/200] Running: What is batch inference and why is it faster?...

🧠 LLM Response:


The TrainedModelInferencer API allows a model to make predictions over a matrix of data in batches. Batching requests to the inferencer produces one or more chunks of response data, which are combined to produce a complete result. Although the total latency is slightly larger, the key benefit the batch inferencer provides is a higher throughput in the number of records that the model can analyze per second.

Splitting up the records into batches, analyzing each batch separately, and then recombining takes longer in real time. However, by requesting that the model analyze records in batches, we effectively eliminate the latency of fetching the data from one wrapper to the other. Regardless of the processing time per record, initiating a TrainedModelInferencer process is more time efficient than repeatedly accessing all 100 records from their original wrapper.

Why batch size matters

A major side-effect of batch processing is latency. Larger batches take longer to process. The trick is to pick a batch size about the same size as a native operation on the GPU or FPGA. Some of our GPU Nvidia Maxwell based cards are reported to have a warp size of 32, so this might be as good as any number to start with. Then there is L1 cache size, register size, core count, and so on.

This is often not an issue for products that aren’t latency sensitive, and don’t have to be near real time. If users click a button and information is pinged back a 100 milliseconds later, perhaps no problem. But, for something like an online game where a user moves a mouse or touches a screen, the expectation is a much shorter sequence between action and result.

The basic trade-offs:

- Reduce the batch size to get lower latency up to a threshold where performance gets affected
- Beware of nonlinear behavior for small batches where network, disk and CPU overhead can mean that smaller is slower
- Increase batch size to get the gains of higher GPU efficiency
- Remember that at large batch sizes, higher latency can alienate the user

Larger batches are most often beneficial, but not always. The trend of deep learning is towards increased accuracy and complexity of models. Greater complexity can decrease the responsiveness of the model per request.

The qualifying SLURM jobs are dispatched in such a way that the batches in the Q are processed first by the oldest job that has not finished, and within a job, the entries in the TXN file are processed in the order they appear—the disadvantage of batching is that all the requests in a batch must wait for the completion of the last record before being dispatched. In the case of longest jobs (Dense convolution jobs mostly), this can be a day.

Any subsequent request that holds the same content as an existing pending Q entry is rejected (TXN file must be unique for records, so records that have been submitted twice are dropped). Models can be scheduled to log out their model output in the format of JSON documents to the log file. The batch inference system uses log collection to compile output and assemble sorted documents for final output.

The request can also specify a minmodels argument to run the inference on a configurable number of models—the Nth model is chosen by selecting evenly spaced records from the training set (every record for model 0, record 2 for model 1, record 4 for model 2, etc.). This behavior is completely orthogonal to the queued batching.

Only up to N published models may be selected, thus the final model in the min models set may be less than N records apart from the previous. This model is distinguished as training over a non-uniform subset of the training records, as it must be, to produce N models. A minmodels value of 3 produces exactly the 0, 2, 4, etc. behavior. The 0th model is still titled as “original” and predicted on every batch inference to insure that the results of the inference are completely non-varying for a single record, but must have the same requestor id and requestor’s IP address, as the requestor and client IP are used to qualify the TXN in the queue so that requests get their correct outputs.

Record consistency (i.e., the 0th model is the largest training set and 1st model is less frequently executed than the 0th, and therefore has a longer relative latency) is achieved by managing the TXN work scheduling such that when a record is processed by any
[144/200] Running: What are rotary sin-cos embeddings?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Rotary embeddings (often referred in the research literature as sinusoidal embeddings) are transformations used for representing data with sequential properties like positions in text. In the case of language modeling embeddings, this means representing $k$ discrete position indices with $k$ $d$-dimensional vectors.

They were first introduced with the Tranformer and used internally by the self-attention mechanism.
We found they they can become especially effective when used for the positional encoding of target output positions, too. Intuitively, this means that if you have a seq-to-seq language model (let's say a model we want to use for machine translation), you can also provide to the decoder the explicit in time information of the tokens it has to generate.
In particular, we used the decoder to generate embeddings in time even for the encoder outputs, that is, instead of providing as input just a stack of hidden states obtained from running a sentence with 3 tokens through the encoder network, we provided it with repeated applications of sin-cos embeddings (one per encoder hidden state) used to generate $3$ representations, the first having the shape of $0$ (padding index) and so on. Since the weights are tied but the input indices varies, we can achieve the embedding of discrete position information in a purely continuous-linear-magnitude space.

We didn't expect to obtain any significant improvement from this technique given what we know about target position embeddings. However, when experimented on WMT machine translation datasets, we saw a modest (on average $5$%) improvement in BLEU, with an increase of speed and memory consumption negligible (as computed with fairseq's *fairnel* pipeline).

We conjecture that this could be due to given the history of previous position tokens (independently from their value), the model can have a better understanding of when to stop producing outputs (for instance, when it encounters repetitions of position representations, as that would signal that the source embeddings are finished, and so it can better decide when to translate the end of sentence token).
Finally, below we provide some implementation details on how to modify fairseq scripts (we plan to open a pull request and make this option available in the standard repository).

How to modify fairseq or use rotary-sin-cos embeddings? We modify three scripts of the defualt pipeline in fairseq.

1. First, we add a (sparse) sin-cos initialization scheme in the positional encoder, apt to represent the same position encoding $k=3$ times (the total number of distinct positions in our hypothesis). You can use the following function and use the output as computational graph for your model:

```
@staticmethod
    def _get_sinusoid_encoding_table(self, seq_length,
                                     embeddings_size):
        # Reference: https://github.com/tensorflow/tensor2tensor
        # https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0893608017300957
        # ----------------------------------- --------
        # YOU CAN ADD THEM IN AN ENCODER NETWORK
        PE = positional_encoding(seq_length, min_timescale=1.0,
                             max_timescale=1.0e4,
                             a_timescale=1.0e4,
                             d_emb=embeddings_size, num_encodings = 3)
        # Definizione dei vettori posizionali di embedding
        return torch.tensor(PE, dtype=torch.float)
```

1. Second, we register those embeddings also for the decoder side with:

```
args.encoder_embed_path and not args.decoder_embed_path: # Changed!!!!!!!!!
    raise FileNotFoundError(
        f"generate_embedding called, but only found encoder embedding: {args.encoder_embed_path}"
    )

if args.encoder_embed_path:
    assert (
        len(args.encoder_embed_path) == 1
    ), "using --sup-embed-path with multiple args is not currently supported"
    encoder_embed_path = args.encoder_embed_path[0]
    encoder_embed_path_dict = get_path_dict(encoder_embed_path)
