[155/200] Running: What is inference latency and why is it important?...

🧠 LLM Response:

Inference latency measures how long it takes from the time a request is made to the time results are returned. It’s a key performance measurement for machine learning and deep learning applications such as real-time video analytics, walk-through metal detectors, and security and monitoring systems using machine learning. The lower the inference latency, the higher the perceived quality of experience (QoE) for a user.

Why does inference latency matter so much?
Traditional 2D video security and surveillance systems are real-time, but adding in machine learning and video analytics only works if your inference latency is sufficiently low (for example, sub-second video analytics is real-time). You won’t be able to make use of advanced machine learning until you can overcome latency problems. This is why our Intel® Movidius™ X @ Intel® Vision event presentations by Luca Lache and Jule Ferneau and Intel® Movidius™ VP Remi El-Ouze got a lot of attention. And then there’s Intel’s next-generation vision processing unit (VPU) featuring the Movidius Myriad™ X Vision Processing Unit (VPU). We’re showing developers how to develop smart cameras and smart devices with <1ms inference latencies.

What else do developers need for near-zero latency?
Remi El-Ouazar recently wrote about Enhancements to Deep Learning Inference on Intel® VPU, mentioning software and performance improvements. Now that we’ve given you a preview on latency optimization and handed you a really amazing Ubuntu 16.04-based development platform (special thanks to the Movidius team), we know you’ll want a convenient way to evaluate performance on different models and network configurations. So we’re sharing an example project here. Of course, we’ll keep innovating to increase the performance of our hardware and the efficiency of our programming model, allowing our customers to reach even greater levels of performance with lower latency.

When will the necessary software tools be available for the Intel Movidius Myriad X VPU?
Our open source VPU Software Development Kit (SDK) and Neural Compute Stick SDK were made available end of January and are continuously updated.
Learn more about development tools and pre-configured evaluation hardware.

Where can I learn more about optimizing for low latency?
We continue to want the world to build with our technology. We’re publishing low latency code optimization resources in our deep learning developer community, and will continue to share additional software tools and videos.
If you want curated updates on our vision technology just scroll down and subscribe.
If you have questions, we're there for you in the Intel® Developer Zone.

