 The solution is int8 (integer) Quantization.

## What is quantization?

In short, we are loosing a little bit of precision (around 10%) while significantly reducing the memory footprint (an order of magnitude). It is not necessary to set out pros and cons because the end user will not notice the difference.

But I am back to my favourite part of computer science: compilers. Being a former developer at Intel I can not pass the question “Why exactly using int8 has a huge footprint?” with strange and important in mind the aspects like not only performance but also power consumption and cost of the hardware.

The answer is that CPUs are performing better with more parallelism. When you "emulate" a float variable on an int8 vector it is also pretty stupid. Sometimes I might be a crazy computer scientist but in my mind, I am thinking of my childhood with Pascal FP numbers and without BN (Batch Normalization). I know Floats (on architecture side) very well but dealing with Int8 I have roughly heard a lot of funny shit with Intel MMX and SSE in 2010. But now it has been a while, old and funny.

## What is int8 quantization?

It is pure computer science from the very low computer graphics (bi-planar OpenGL Pixel FMT), there is no magic there — more or less the same as lossy data decompression algorithms. In short — “do more with less”.

You can enable quantization simplicity using gradio as introduced earlier.

## Quantization is the default procedure for huggingface inference pipelines

In the case of Transformers `AutoModel` is now adding quantization for the model can be quantized by the following code.

As we can see, for the relatively small (7B) model, it has reduced the memory footprint by twice and for the large models, it is even more. In any case, it does not matter what model do you use, it has been already fine made to be already fine with the 8-bit dynamic computation:

How does it manage to do so? The technique called quantization The relevant GitHub issue can be found here. It can be persisted again in the pipeline with `save_pretrained()` in order to reuse later or persist in ONNX or tensorRT formats.

## Inference performance benefits

The quantization brought significant performance improvements for threaded LLaMA models. Not only that but also these improvements were even greater when coupled with quantization of both the model weights and the inputs.

## Why quantization is important?

In our case, quantization is benefiting from lower memory consumption, which eventually leads to tangible (does not consume that much memory or GPU VRAM) and substantial compute performance improvements. In short, it can be scaled nowhere for a shorter period of time.

But it depends on the particular requirements. However, a good example of quantization usage is for an embedded system in medical devices for a proper “security zone” on the hardware side.

## More formal explanation of what is quantization?

It is a technique for compressing super large models and reducing inference time. See the paper published by Google Brain and other quantization methods like Kick The Gates And Lose The Float in which researchers from Amazon reported superior performance in Transformers.

Earlier, I have showcased a popular example of quantizing large dataset of TeraByte scale specifically for Chat models - FaceBook Cassandra and Intel Signal Processing Library. And a popular video of Eli Bendersky which I have aired more than a decade ago in Yandex Class in Moscow.

## Step-by-step tutorial

The full tutorial can be found written by NVIDIA researchers How to deploy GPT using a very old-school language — Pascal. Ouah that will be really, really cool and for sure I will be following it too but it is really out of scope as regards to my current project of making production-ready ChatGPT - Atomnet.

## Conclusion

Quantization for low overload is a blessing for ChatGPT, and is covered within Huggingface Transformers.
