[160/200] Running: What is warmup time in inference pipelines?...

🧠 LLM Response:


After scheduling a deployment in inference, you may have noticed that the API is in the “initializing” status for a few seconds.  This is otherwise known as “warm-up”.  Why this is needed and how is it applied are the topics of this next blog entry.

Some machine learning models are composed of complex network structures which require several instructions and possibly several memory swaps for their first few steps after the deployed API is called.  These first few calls or batches of calls are often referred to as “cold calls” because, after the first few seconds of the deployment’s life, each successive instruction requires less time to perform than the previous (but some may even take the same amount of time as the first) until the entire model starts to perform calls/batches consistently over time (known as “steady state”).

To prepare Vizit’s generative AI engines for a consistent and predictable performance, each NLP API is trained (outlined in the entire inference process here) which prepares and closes the model down before moving from training into the inference state.  In the inference state, the model is reopened and prepared for inference through its owning generative AI engines, which may also involves opening the pre-loaded models on the GPUs prior to inference testing.

Ultimately, the warmup call is quick to execute and is particularly useful when predicting simple calls that help achieve consistent timing measurements during an engine’s tenure.  We talk more about how this is done in the next blog entry.
[161/200] Running: How to stream JSON tokens using curl?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 By Daniel C. Sobral (clojurecircles) [timestamp]

There are many scenarios where you want to stream JSON tokens instead of a whole parsed JSON document. I recently had a data source that could only be accessed via a REST endpoint with JSON output. The file it produced was a 7+ GiBs and there’s no way to process that in memory.

You might think of, say, `jq` for this, but the developer of `jq` considered streaming to be out of scope: https://github.com/stedolan/jq/blob/master/docs/issues.md

Turns out this is not only possible, but very easy (at least as long as you’re happy to hardcode JSON key names in your command). The trick is combining curl and jq while ensuring curl is streaming, and while providing answers to jq that guide the stream parsing.

The curl command does basically two things: downloads a webpage, and prints it to stdout. If the URL is a REST endpoint and JSON is the data format, we could save the output to a disk file for latter analysis.

We can, however, stream this version of “the webpage” instead of saving it from curl. So what we do is press everything between curl and the webserver (from curl’s point of view) and press everything between curl and the disk (from the filesystem point of view):

```
$ curl https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JSON | jq '.'
```

Of course, the curl output here isn’t exactly JSON, it has HTML tags, markdown, etc. We can go, instead, to a real JSON webpage, GraqQL API explorer for instance:

```
$ curl https://graphql.banana.com/graphql -H 'content-type: application/json' --data '{"query":"query {\n  viewer {\n    user {\n      login\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}' | jq '.'
```

If you run that, you’ll get stdout data from jq but it’ll all come at once, in a precomputed fashion (you’ll see no sign of the `>>` prompt when you try it). Having it all at once doesn’t help streaming of course, but then again, calling jq with `jq '.'` isn’t anything more useful than running `cat` here. The benefit of jq here is that if we can start getting low level JSON output, we’ll be able to parse it iteratively.

The magic happens when you make the jq call iterative instead of non-iterative. For example, for the graphql JSON endpoint above, you could call it like this:

```
$ curl https://graphql.banana.com/graphql -H 'content-type: application/json' --data '{"query":"query {\n  viewer {\n    user {\n      login\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}' | jq --stream -c 'fromstream(1|truncate_stream(inputs)) | .[0]'
```

Here, the output comes in chunks. The output you get is (without the red line numbering, inserted here just to call attention to the :data portion of the output):

> 1 {“user”:{“login”:”jbrownlie”}}
> 2 {“login”:”jbrownlie”}
> 3 “data”
> 4 {“viewer”:{“user”:{“login”:”jbrownlie”}}}

(the output can be in different order…)

Notice that the first three lines of output represent incrementally smaller and smaller chunks of the JSON, amounting to at least two invocations of the output expression (ie. running `jq .[]`) for each of these lines.

The recursive structure is given by graphql semantics (involved in the quoted query expression) while the jq options -c and --stream direct you start processing the JSON data as the curl input becomes available.

For more on this, check: https://www.reddit.com/r/commandline/comments/527vyd/how_to_parse_large_json_files_from_the_command/d7iaq4s/
