At Datashuttle, we love dask. Every day we peak under the hood and read every english verse written on the library, but there are always new things to learn and discover.

Recently I was looking into the `generate_with_streaming()` wrapper.

As seen in the Dask Tutorials Jupyter Notebook this wrapper helps when a operation being fed into dask takes more time to setup than it takes to run.

I found this function very interesting so I thought it would be a helpful exercise trying to explain how it works.

The idea is that when we are dealing with datasets that are larger than a single machine’s resources, we may have a function in our code that doesn’t require the full dataset to run but still needs a lot of time to prepare all the code underneath.

This snippet was originally posted on https://stackoverflow.com/questions/54744788/dask-dataframe-using-generate-wrapper

Now generating dask dataframes with an example that wraps the `generate_with_streaming()` is easy as pie, maybe even easier.

```
@ds.pack(generate_with_streaming(some_helpful_function, some_extra_processing, **my_s3_params)
def generate_with_streaming_function(*args):
    return some_helpful_function(*args)
```

`pack_s3` is now able to wrap `generate_with_streaming` functions and make your developer life a blessed day.

This example shows how you can get to get smaller concatenated snippets of data out of a distributed source.

At Datashuttle, we want to make the process of writing data pipelines easier, letting you focus on the code instead of the plumbing.
