[164/200] Running: What are server cold starts in model serving?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Today, we’ll explore some of the main ideas behind serverless inference.

There are many reasons run your models on a server using cloud compute. But to get the most from your infrastructure, there’s a bit to learn about how today’s architectures work, and how they’re best-suited for deep learning inference. We’ll start by taking a look at cold starts.

So, let’s get into it!

## TL;DR

- An inference or prediction server lets you use your trained machine learning models in production
- The emerging ‘serverless’ architecture runs each request and response cycle in its own isolated container or as an isolated function
- However, this means requests could be ‘cold started’ where the instance is not ready for service: a server would have to be provisioned and a container or function instantiated
- You can use multiprocess pre-serving or pre-warming to avoid cold starts for serverless models
- Some serverless inference services have automatic warming built-in

## What is a server cold start?

A ‘cold start’ is a server loading or starting needed runtime software when it receives a request. This can only happen on the first request in any application instance’s request cycle (e.g. for a long-used container, it could be the next request-response cycle)

So, unlike a typical web server – which is always running and is always considered ‘warm’ – a serverless computing implementation wouldn’t be warm, or would have to ‘cold start’ when receiving a request.

Serverless computing means we’re working with a cloud architecture that provisions – allocates – resources for each request to a given compute server in any request-response cycle. Its opposite is a dedicated container or even VM for every container. With serverless, we’re effectively working with on-demand compute resources.

These can only be allocated, of, for each request. In turn, you pay for only what you actually use, and scale seamlessly as workloads demand.

Serverless inference means that revolves around putting models at the heart of your applications’ infrastructure. It also means it’s up to you to optimize those ‘cold starts’ to avoid high latency and to conserve extra compute costs.

As a sidebar, the ‘serverless’ moniker isn’t entirely accurate. There is a server and provisioning of compute resources – it’s just done automatically and relatively cost-efficiently. So, in practice, there’s a cold start for virtual machines or containers that are spun up by a particular provider’s serverless computing service.

## How to avoid serverless cold starts

Tip 1: Pre-serving

The pre-serving approach says the client can send an automated ‘ping’ to the service for a request. The server’s API endpoint and the client both run a script for the model endpoint on each model API being served. This script executes regularly and is set to a timeout.

Since the service then makes a request to itself, the container is serverless infrastructure can spin-up and prepare itself. This isn’t all that neat, and it doesn’t mean that there can’t be some downtime in between hosting the model and actually serving a request.

The problem is that the service can be complicated to configure and to maintain. But for the right use cases – perhaps notably in business scenarios – you can run this script on a daily schedule but hit your quota quickly. Just remember that there will reliably always be a cold-start on your first request.

However you can ensure that all requests reach your server without any latency, and you can also track which of your endpoints are hitting their time limits. You can resolve this by increasing the serverless infrastructure region, and running your models on GPUs or other specialized hardware.

Tip 2: Pre-warming

Alternatively, we can try ‘pre-warming’ your model for deployment. Just make sure to allocate – or provision – enough runtime resources for whichever serverless architecture you’re working on.

The script is kept running long enough for the API to scale up. You can then push an increasingly large number of requests through the API so that it can warm up. It can only warm up by responding, so you’re essentially initiating extra work for it to respond directly to. In practice, you should run a dummy request to the API.

This method is very effective, but it can result in overuse penalties from some service providers for running all the dummy requests through the API endpoint. But it
[165/200] Running: Explain how to avoid padding inefficiencies....

🧠 LLM Response:


Padding consists of adding extra bytes of data to the end of the last block to ensure that the data is a multiple of a cipher's block size. Padding always occurs, even if the data's total length already is a multiple of the block size.

In theory, handlers from different vendors could use different types of padding. However, most vendors use the type defined in PKCS#7 (or PKCS#5, which is equivalent) because it's the most complete. See The Padded Message and Padding in the article PKCS#5 Encryption for the definition.

Implementations of this method must support the PKCS#5 format.

A problem with padding is that it changes the length of the output (by at most 16 bytes if the block size is 16, for example). The message length must be included in the plaintext somehow, and changing the length of the ciphertext is the only straightforward way to do this. An alternative, more efficient scheme is to use ciphertext stealing.
[166/200] Running: How does tokenizer pre-processing affect TTFT?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Won't tokenizers like RFX be only as fast as the underlying processor?

Tokenization provides two orthogonal features:

1: Fixed latency instruction operands.

2: Preprocessing during decode.

With RFX, the product of # registers and max_iOperand_size is fixed, thus offering a fixed latency operand compared to GPR usage. The preprocess is only limited by the underlying processor.

With RFX, how does cycle time come out to be in the ~500ps range, and what keeps it from being as fast as it can physically be? Any tile restrictions/diameter in terms of regular layout constraints?

In a GPR-only architecture, the clock is driven by GPR read port speed. Typically, most GPRs need a PPAX architecture which limits read to one GPR per operation. Since RFX tokens generate many results at a time, this read port restriction no longer impacts the design which allows the base architecture to take better advantage of higher frequency operations. The custom RFX architectures should then be able to operate at whatever the underlying NP-based microarchitecture can support.

As a thought exercise, I am assuming two Pipelined GPR registers per xyz operand -- memory of that seems plenty dense in highly-regular vertically oriented tile. Or possibly two highly dense memory banks per operand. The latency through a single register or bank would exceed single cycle but for some bizarro case where one might demand many unpipelined GPR or unpipelined double-banking, then the RFX may be more compact. I suppose that if there is some constraint on GPR spacing and thus having two Pipelined GPR per operand being slow enough to make it too big, then that would make a primary argument for operands.

The primary argument for RFX is the ability to eliminate entire pipeline stages by the preprocessing capability. The comparison to GPR given within the TFETs paper is a worst-case scenario since other RFX architectures can further simplify the FU front end.

For GPR, assuming no dielectric cap, which becomes self-limiting before length - effective dielectric increases with overlap, IM2, and timing degrades due to charge sharing. It's easy enough to flip GPR in-place. There may be an overlap to offset FoM and density if that is the weak sister. Compared to memory, GPR register and restore are potentially lower energy of a flip.

Handling coherency between tiles or sub-tiles may also be a challenge compared to GPR.  If GPR is ordered decode, that self-manages coherency mostly but can also be done in a single clock by design of dependent control. Comparing to data fabrics, GPR is more tightly coupled than what could be made with memory.

GPRs are most vulnerable to leaky transistors even with lowering the voltage for inactive lines.  Keeping the data valid is very costly, but cost is zero clock for a flip.
