[171/200] Running: How does model size affect inference latency?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Using a variety of models, from image classification, to object detection, to NLP and more, let’s explore trade-offs to be considered when selecting models. There are also more examples in my full presentation video at ReWork ReCoCo.

NOTE: All numbers derived on the Azure cloud. Nvidia has a tensorRT which optimizes models. Lots of work has been done in this space, and these results will not be identical. This is just one demonstration of there being interesting decisions to be made.

Background: GPU Lanes

To understand model size/speed trade-offs, let’s look through the lens of GPU lanes. Lanes, at a very high level, are how wide the GPU is. When we do a parallel operation, like multiplying two matrices, we assign each result a lane. Side note: adding one through a row requires a sequence of operations, not parallel.

This is important, because smaller models will occupy less lanes. We don’t actually get the speed increases we might expect. So two models: half and twice the height may be similar in runtime performance.

We want to stay focused on the right part of the curve: model sizes which achieve critical performance in a reasonable amount of time. There are large gains as we move to larger models, way to the right of these. By not moving too far up this curve, we have large performance gains.

Architecture Speeds

EfficientNet comes in multiple versions, allowing us to test different speeds.

It’s actually fairly linear. What happens if we compare architectures? We expect ResNet50 and EfficientNetB4 to have similar performance. Le’s compare these, along with ResNet18.

For the core model, we go from close to 4 seconds for resnet18, resnet50, and EfficientNetB4 to over 10 seconds for EfficientNetB7.

We can see this across multiple experiments. There does appear to be a step function at the high end. But we can see there are efficiency cutoffs.

Object Detection Model Sizes

When looking at object detection models, there are also multiple model sizes. Larger models both improves accuracy, and speed? How does the pipeline perform overall over the instance? Turns out, we have a flat runtime and size pairing for object detection.

NLP Models

As we look at NLP models, we see even more concretely the size jump at the high end. This is after reduce_to_fit by gpt2.

This is congruent with the other sizes we’ve seen, and where we get top performance. Looking over all the models on my workstation with four RTX 3090’s.

We see a huge jump in accuracy above 60B parameters (60^9 weights). We see the speed jump occurs at 1000B parameters, so that’s not advised for those with limited time frames.
