[178/200] Running: What are temperature vs randomness in sampling?...

🧠 LLM Response:


When a sample is drawn, let’s say with a stochastic function such as Metropolis-Hastings, we measure relative propensity of its samples to over- or under-represent some regions of a sampling distribution. Tempering refers to a slow sampling iteration where samples are barely accepted. To understand tempering it’s useful to recall optimization as exploitation, or precising ones bet—as close to a measure of probability as one can get. Tempering, by contrast, is exploration, a recondite root of probability drawn from uncertainty. Tempering, in this case, also gradually increases (from cold to hot) and decreases (from hot to cold) sample entropy as iterations are proposed. Generally speaking, temperature adjustment in sampling methodologies acts by slow functions moving a proposal distribution of a sampling function such as Metropolis-Hastings. Once the broad bounds of a sampling distribution are roughly sampled, a simulated annealing-like (tempering) procedure can adjust the relative propensity of sampling different regions (entropy) before slowly relaxing (cool) the sample distribution towards a more probable multidimensional measure (cool).

This procedure, generally, can be thought of as the a broadening the “tails” or extremities of a predictive distribution – first, we sample. We have some confidence in that sampling process, secondarily, we adjust the range and temperatures of our sampling distribution, then finally we slowly let our distribution cool to a narrower range, arriving at our canonical distribution in regions with the highest probability. When we initialize a sample(s) we don’t know where this process will take us nor where it started – a sample’s historical training is lost to information divergence (information entropy).

A region of a sampling distribution may have high information content, as such the probability (or surface height of a distribution) in that region will also be higher. Entropy adjustment (tempering) helps us to resolve point(s) of probable convergence during the lifetime (number of iterations) of a sampler, relaxing as convergence is inevitable. What is this like, as a user of these algorithms? Think of it this way: turn on a sampler. What is the distribution here—not much. So let’s adjust the temperature of our sampling region by, perhaps, increasing the range of our sampling pool – let change the step size to 100,000 meters off the ground.

Conceptual drawing of a temperature jump.

Big moves explore more. Generally in MCMC so long as your range is not over-sampling or under-sampling your distribution (with a known or well-estimated sigma, say), a step can be more or less broad, with the caveat that narrowing, at the outset, will help you get on to finding the right range (or intentionally keeping the step size small will help you efficiently probe the surface of what you assume is a close-in, easily explorable distribution). Once converged the acceptance ratio of a sampler will crash. At that point, the step size will mostly impact the acceptance ratio of your sampler – of course, there’s always the chance the input data were just taken from a random sample (doubtful after enough concatenated calls to a sampler, but theoretically possible). When your step is too small, during exploration, it is possible to overestimate the number of steps. With enough data, your marginal may demonstrate something else, some prior (expectation that measurements may have come from a normal, really, rather than uniform distribution [histogram of sales from January through September?]), because entropy-like jumps are most useful when regions of a prediction have been poorly under-sampled. Programming languages and languages like Stan and PyMC3 have shortcuts, and often simple settings, to understand and adjust entropy and accept at the sampler level for functions such as Metropolis-Hastings. When we think about a distribution that’s been under-sample it’s quite possible to over-adjust our step size – we may be quite well offscale but we need to increase sample entropy (step) or accept a region that’s just not well-sampled (which will exaggerate the posterior p.d.f., in case you’re wondering what Misleading Posteriors is all about, how accurate are the guide RNAs [given uncertainty in that distribution], 1 billion cells are orders of magnitude greater than usual 10,000 cell CRISPR/Cas9 modifications…).
