[181/200] Running: Explain the internals of HF `generate()` loop....

🧠 LLM Response:


What is the HF `generate()` loop?

## 1 Answer

The Haskell For Mac OSX FunctionAppLab is an example application to demonstrate live-code literate programming environments and one component of that is the definition of the re-gen(eration) loop that creates each composition and all its future is instances. This loop is driven by the definition of Applicative (of Data.Functor.) You can find this definition within lib.hs or link to the contents of the above at GitHub.

The Applicative interface is defined like so:

```
class Functor f => Applicative f where
    pure :: a -> f a
    (<*>) :: f (a -> b) -> f a -> f b
```

The interface between Functor and Applicative is quite clean, being built on consumption of a traversable Functor structure like `Maybe (a -> b) -> Maybe a -> Maybe b` which indicates that the `Maybe` type signature for `a -> b` could be interpretted as a transform of `a` to `b`.

I basically started using this to work backwards and disassemble Functor and derive the definition of Applicative. My motivation was to better understand code/symbol type automatic generation. Because HF is registering of symbols within the runtime, and writing of all subsequent generated symbols to disk, my solution is to take the monadic process of running `eval` or `fold`, or by extension, `sequence`, and `join` on `StateT s a` (or similiarly `ST s`) and rewrite the monad’s evaluation loop as a pure function of type `I -> I` where `I` is `IO s` and `s` is of the `Data.Functor` class.

The objective is to have a pure function to derive a Monad `m a` as a pure function of itself. My solution for finding and proving how to do this was accomplished by wireframing the general evaluation setup for GHC and applying the general lens of Applicative in each state transform spot, like `StateT s a -> s -> (a, s)`. Putting this wireframe under an `StateT s` monad comes quite easy and a transform from `StateT s` to `Data.Functor.Functor I` is trivial. As a result, I coordinated a definition of a minimalistic virtual “function machine” that uses the above purity of `I -> I` to define all operations doing the evaluation state of the aforementioned monad. That allowed me to rewrite Monads over `IO` (and the transitive `*` state transformers) as well as hierarchies of “function machines” representing a Monad’s stepping process. Thus I can show exactly where the Apiplicative occurs and how it is implementation occurs within the runtime itself. Look for more examples to be released via the HF platform.

I plan to release more code samples of more complex functions and application source code using the HF platform on the GitHub. Each will include the `*Machine.hs` files deriving the Monad types and implementations for the “function machine.”

Keep learning @KateAndersen, it’s important to know how to automate the building blocks FOR your runtime processing.

There’s API(s) which are undocumented in the module headers above, but can be found in the module wrappers.

