🧠 LLM Response:


1 Like

Block size (number of transactions included per block) relates to latency in the following sense.

With every block created and appended to the blockchain, the confirmed state of all contracts has to be updated, and this is computationally expensive. We could simply ignore that and treat this cost as being "amortized" over the entire set of blocks produced, but the problem with this strategy is that it creates a strong incentive for validators to limit block size. As the blocksize is an on-chain parameter controlled via the governance process, validators are free to change it as they see fit in the presence of congestion. The higher the block size, the larger the cost of appending blocks. The cost is not just that of computational overhead, but also the opportunity cost of the downtime during which contracts cannot be invoked. During this downtime compared to a cryptocurrency, much less economic activity is occurring, reducing the network’s utilization (the “switching cost problem”). A low switching cost would strongly incentivize validators to keep the block size low, thus achieving lower latency at the expense of throughput.

The latency overhead of a blocksize of (e.g.) 4000 transactions per block, is the net result of the latency of a 4000-tx block vs the latency reduction due to the stronger incentive the validators now have for increasing throughput. Depending on how this trade-off plays out, it’s possible that a larger blocks generating more complexity and switching costs has a lower overall latency. This all depends on how latency-vs-batch size effects various aspects, and is a very complicated and context-dependent analysis. I’m afraid I don’t know specific good models for this.

One additional approach that’s been proposed on our forum is a system that allows updatable contracts to merge multiple unrelated operations into a single large instruction, thus constraining the complexity toward the cost of invoking merkle validators and away from the cost of updating merkle updates due to multiple unrelated instructions. There are some architectural difficulties with this, primarily being that the cost of verifying updatable contracts is quite high as a contract author cannot simply state the transform – rather they must provide an executable sCrypt that performs the transform, and also all ancestors of that updatable contract must also be presented when the contract is invoked so that contract clients can determine whether the updatable contract is in an unspent state.
