In part 1 of this series, we explored one of the practical applications of the NLP (natural language processing) approach known as Transfer Learning (TL). We demonstrated that TL efficiently utilizes the knowledge gained from solving a problem and applies it to a different but related one. Our results showed that the modification of a pre-trained model’s classifier during fine-tuning can and will lead to improvements in classification accuracy. Our model was trained to classify reviews from different categories, and the results demonstrate increased applicability of a TL approach.

In this post, we would now like to focus on modifying the Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) basic structure of the layer embedding transform structure to classify our dataset, as mentioned in our previous post,  using a module proposed by Glorot et al in CNN’s for sentence classification (Link). As was done previously, MLP layers are created after obtaining the sentence’s embedding in order to obtain classification results and performance metrics.

# A complex MLP layer

As seen in the figure above, the MLP structure was modified using 2 filters arising from the embeddings generated per sentence or text in the pre-trained BERT model, allowing for 2 different representations of sentence-level embedding to be analyzed by the future classifier. Concretely, these filters account for the forward and backwards embedding data sequence using windows of 3. The results obtained are merged layerwise, forming 2 layers as seen in the figure above. The classifier results are obtained using these concatenations: the embedding merged forward and backwards.

The relationship between layer size and the dataset data types

In our first analysis, we obtained good results using a simple MLP layer built from embedding created by the BERT model. As established in our previous post, we would like to implement a second layer to continue improving potential accuracy.

The table above shows the results of the accuracy metrics: a significant increase in accuracy in the training set, as well as in the accuracy validation. The loss metrics indicate that learning has improved compared to the baseline value of 0. The ROC curve is also favorable.

In contrast, results in the test set are also very interesting.

It can be seen in the table above that accuracy levels are significantly higher than the baseline. The AUC values are also positive, especially in comparison to values obtained previously.

# Conclusions

In the same way as with the pre-trained BERT layer, we can conclude that a second more complicated MLP layer improves metrics regarding the backwards training set and the validation set. ROC AUC values are shown to be similarly optimized as in our first experiment. In the same way, a simple MLP layer shows its high potential to be utilized in real applications, as CNN layers require much more computation at the derivative cost function level and training.

In our next post, we will explore a combination of the RNN (recurrent neural network) structures obtained using Transfer Learning through a  ULMFiT TL, as proposed by Howard et al (Link).

