🧠 LLM Response:


Convolutional neural networks classify images by running them through a number of different filters which detect features such as lines and edges. These features are combined in complex ways until large-scale features such as objects are detected. Convolutional neural networks are using feature maps in their intermediate layers. During training, a convolutional neural network finds the parameters for the filters automatically.

Convolutional neural networks differ from basic feed-forward networks by the addition of convolution layers, pooling layers which are sometimes called down-sampling layers, and un-pooling layers which are sometimes called up-sampling layers. Convolution layers apply filters to the features maps. Pooling layers summarize the features contained in regions of a feature map. The addition of a pooling layer helps to protect against overfitting, although some programs drop the pooling layer altogether. Un-pooling layers help to increase the sizes of feature maps.

Convolutional neural networks benefit (as do other neural networks) from the use of the rectified linear activation function (ReLu) as a transfer function between layers. Without the ReLu, it’s much harder to train a convolutional neural network to achieve good results.
